WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.015
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4550.

2
00:00:01.015 --> 00:00:02.029
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:04.397
<v REBECCA COOPER>Okay, so today is December seventh, 2015.

4
00:00:04.397 --> 00:00:11.162
I am Becca Cooper, representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with the LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:11.162 --> 00:00:17.250
Today we are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

6
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:23.000
I'm here with Mrs. Audrey Sims at her home in Lake Charles . . . Moss Bluff . . . Lake Charles?

7
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:23.769
<v AUDREY SIMS GUILLORY>Lake Charles [Louisiana].

8
00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:25.051
<v COOPER>We are in Lake Charles.

9
00:00:25.051 --> 00:00:26.846
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

10
00:00:26.846 --> 00:00:28.385
This is our first interview together . . .

11
00:00:28.385 --> 00:00:29.154
<v SIMS>Yes, it is.

12
00:00:29.154 --> 00:00:30.179
<v COOPER>. . . which is very exciting.

13
00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:33.000
Can you please state your full name for the recording?

14
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.400
<v SIMS>Audrey Mae Sims Guillory.

15
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.000
<v COOPER>And what year were you born?

16
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:39.600
<v SIMS>1947.

17
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.000
<v COOPER>Where were you born?

18
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.800
<v SIMS>Lake Charles, Louisiana.

19
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.000
<v COOPER>And do you have any siblings?

20
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.000
<v SIMS>Yes.

21
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
I'm the oldest of five.

22
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.000
<v COOPER>What are your siblings' names?

23
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:14.000
<v SIMS>Oscar Sims Junior, Myrtle Ray Sims Brown, Shelby Brown Junior, and Barbara Fay Sims Johnson.

24
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.000
<v COOPER>And what are your parents' names?

25
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.000
<v SIMS>Mary Beverly Sims and Oscar Sims.

26
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.000
<v COOPER>So Beverly is her maiden name?

27
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
<v SIMS>Yes.

28
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.468
<v COOPER>And where are they from?

29
00:01:37.468 --> 00:01:41.629
<v SIMS>My father was from Gueydan, Louisiana.

30
00:01:41.629 --> 00:01:46.484
And my mother was from Branch, Louisiana.

31
00:01:46.484 --> 00:01:52.726
<v COOPER>And how did they end up coming to Mossville?

32
00:01:52.726 --> 00:02:09.371
<v SIMS>We went to Mossville because my uncle, which is my momma's brother, was the pastor of the Methodist church in Westlake [Louisiana] and Mossville.

33
00:02:09.371 --> 00:02:13.532
<v COOPER>And what year did you get . . .

34
00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:17.000
<v SIMS>In 1959 to 1965.

35
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.186
<v COOPER>And what were your parents doing when you came to Mossville?

36
00:02:22.186 --> 00:02:29.257
<v SIMS>My mom, by this time, was a single mom because she had divorced my father.

37
00:02:29.257 --> 00:02:32.557
And she was a single, working mom.

38
00:02:32.557 --> 00:02:36.800
<v COOPER>Where did she . . . What did she do for work?

39
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.986
<v SIMS>She was a housekeeper [Sims clarifies that she was a maid].

40
00:02:41.986 --> 00:02:44.814
<v COOPER>Do you remember where she worked?

41
00:02:44.814 --> 00:02:49.057
<v SIMS>For the Earl Daughenbaugh family of Lake Charles, Louisiana.

42
00:02:49.057 --> 00:02:50.000
<v COOPER>Okay.

43
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.250
<v SIMS>And she had been working for them before I was born.

44
00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.318
<v COOPER>And your father lived in Mossville, too?

45
00:02:55.318 --> 00:02:57.091
<v SIMS>No, he lived in Lake Charles.

46
00:02:57.091 --> 00:02:58.864
<v COOPER>So he never moved to Mossville?

47
00:02:58.864 --> 00:03:00.636
<v SIMS>No, he never moved to Mossville.

48
00:03:00.636 --> 00:03:03.000
<v COOPER>What did he do in Lake Charles?

49
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.838
<v SIMS>He was disabled.

50
00:03:04.838 --> 00:03:12.803
He was a legally blind sibling of a twin, and he was disabled.

51
00:03:12.803 --> 00:03:17.092
<v COOPER>And did your grandparents live in Mossville?

52
00:03:17.092 --> 00:03:24.444
<v SIMS>No, my grandparents are only . . . so my grandfather on my mom's side.

53
00:03:24.444 --> 00:03:35.472
My father's mother died of giving birth to a set of twins, again, when he was about four.

54
00:03:35.472 --> 00:03:47.113
His father died before I was born, so I never got to see my grandparents on my father's side.

55
00:03:47.113 --> 00:04:00.592
I only got to see one grandparent, which was my mother's father that died a month after I was married in '66.

56
00:04:00.592 --> 00:04:14.070
But I didn't see my grandmother because my mom's mom was a mother of fifteen, and she died at fifty-two of cancer.

57
00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:14.683
<v COOPER>Wow.

58
00:04:14.683 --> 00:04:25.099
<v SIMS>So I never got to see . . . That's why I wanted to be a great-grandmother to my grandkids.

59
00:04:25.099 --> 00:04:30.000
<v COOPER>And how many kids do you have?

60
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:45.316
<v SIMS>I have two: a boy and a girl, Rhonda Renee Guillory James and Rodrick Rollin Guillory, and they are seven years apart.

61
00:04:45.316 --> 00:04:51.582
Rhonda has two children, two girls: Jasmine and [Jaliyah?].

62
00:04:51.582 --> 00:04:55.759
And my son has seven children.

63
00:04:55.759 --> 00:04:56.456
<v COOPER>Wow.

64
00:04:56.456 --> 00:05:04.810
<v SIMS>And they are Christopher, Taylor, Sydney, Rawlin, Kelan, and Kalin, and Ryan.

65
00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:05.506
<v COOPER>Wow.

66
00:05:05.506 --> 00:05:08.291
That's a big family.

67
00:05:08.291 --> 00:05:14.557
So what did you know about your . . . the one

68
00:05:14.557 --> 00:05:25.000
<c.p>grandparent that you did . . . that you were in touch with when you were little?

69
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.637
<v SIMS>He was a person to be looked up to.

70
00:05:30.637 --> 00:05:34.395
He was very . . . not very religious;

71
00:05:34.395 --> 00:05:45.668
he was . . . strong faith in God that taught his children that way because of them in the generations,

72
00:05:45.668 --> 00:05:55.689
there were one minister that he fathered and the cousins, the next generation, there's seven ministers.

73
00:05:55.689 --> 00:06:01.953
And we all got that from being under his supervision.

74
00:06:01.953 --> 00:06:08.842
<v COOPER>So when you came to Mossville you were around twelve, right?

75
00:06:08.842 --> 00:06:11.347
Does that add up?

76
00:06:11.347 --> 00:06:15.105
<v SIMS>Yes, about ten or twelve . . . eleven.

77
00:06:15.105 --> 00:06:20.742
<v COOPER>What do you remember of first moving to Mossville?

78
00:06:20.742 --> 00:06:30.137
<v SIMS>When I first moved I was very lonely, because my mom and father had divorced.

79
00:06:30.137 --> 00:06:48.300
And when I went to the elementary part—that's where I was—I walked to school with a young girl that had come to this town a month before I did.

80
00:06:48.300 --> 00:07:00.826
I wanted to be her friend, and when I would speak to her in the morning, she would ignore me.

81
00:07:00.826 --> 00:07:12.726
So I wrote her a note when I got to school and said, "I want to be your friend.

82
00:07:12.726 --> 00:07:15.858
Will you be my friend?"

83
00:07:15.858 --> 00:07:24.000
We became friends until she left my sophomore year in high school.

84
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.845
<v COOPER>And what was her name?

85
00:07:25.845 --> 00:07:27.690
<v SIMS>Her name was Katherine [LeBleu?].

86
00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:30.274
<v COOPER>[LeBleu?] . . . So was she your neighbor, too?

87
00:07:30.274 --> 00:07:32.488
<v SIMS>Yes, she was in my neighborhood.

88
00:07:32.488 --> 00:07:35.440
<v COOPER>Do you remember who else lived around you?

89
00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:41.345
<v SIMS>It was nobody in that neighborhood but she and I and the siblings of my uncle.

90
00:07:41.345 --> 00:07:42.821
He had six children.

91
00:07:42.821 --> 00:07:50.940
And we initially, when we first got there, we moved in with them until we could find a house of our own.

92
00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:51.310
<v COOPER>Wow.

93
00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:55.000
So there were eleven kids living in one house.

94
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:55.450
<v SIMS>Yes.

95
00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:58.600
Eleven kids, three adults and one house.

96
00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:01.300
<v COOPER>And what was your uncle's name?

97
00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:01.750
<v SIMS>D.S.

98
00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:03.100
Beverly . . . Reverend D.S.

99
00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:03.550
Beverly.

100
00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:04.450
<v COOPER>Reverend D.S.

101
00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:04.900
Beverly.

102
00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:05.350
<v SIMS>D.S.

103
00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:05.800
Beverly.

104
00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:13.000
<v COOPER>And what street did you all live on when you first moved into their house?

105
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.042
<v SIMS>Flower Street.

106
00:08:14.042 --> 00:08:17.689
<v COOPER>What do you remember about the house?

107
00:08:17.689 --> 00:08:19.772
How did it look?

108
00:08:19.772 --> 00:08:27.066
<v SIMS>It was bigger than I was accustomed of seeing a house to live in,

109
00:08:27.066 --> 00:08:34.359
because like I said, I moved a lot in my lifetime and my childhood.

110
00:08:34.359 --> 00:08:44.257
I put it in my memoirs as being the kid in the neighborhood that was a neighbor to everybody,

111
00:08:44.257 --> 00:08:50.509
because every month, or every two or three weeks, we were moving.

112
00:08:50.509 --> 00:09:00.407
So this house being a big house was something great for me because we always had little one-room . . . one-bedroom,

113
00:09:00.407 --> 00:09:03.533
three-room houses that I lived in.

114
00:09:03.533 --> 00:09:08.743
<v COOPER>And why were . . . why was that that you kept moving?

115
00:09:08.743 --> 00:09:12.389
<v SIMS>Because of financial hardships for my mom.

116
00:09:12.389 --> 00:09:22.808
They would let her stay as long as they could, as long as she can afford to pay the rent.

117
00:09:22.808 --> 00:09:28.539
She wasn't a begging woman, but she was a proud woman.

118
00:09:28.539 --> 00:09:40.000
And when she knew that she couldn't afford to meet the requirements of what they set up, we had to move.

119
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.679
<v COOPER>And where else did you live in Mossville?

120
00:09:44.679 --> 00:09:48.774
<v SIMS>The streets are [Pulman?] . . . Self . . . not Self.

121
00:09:48.774 --> 00:09:52.283
What's that . . . it's not Prater Road.

122
00:09:52.283 --> 00:09:54.623
Old Spanish Trail [laughs].

123
00:09:54.623 --> 00:10:03.396
<v COOPER>Is there one particular house that you remember more than the others, or that you

124
00:10:03.396 --> 00:10:06.906
<c.p>spent more time growing up in?

125
00:10:06.906 --> 00:10:12.170
<v SIMS>The one house I really remember, it was beautiful.

126
00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:22.698
I had my own room and I dressed it up and fixed it up because Mom let me.

127
00:10:22.698 --> 00:10:32.642
That was my reward for being the oldest, watching the younger ones and taking care of them.

128
00:10:32.642 --> 00:10:43.755
I . . . begun preparing the holiday festivities at the age of twelve, Thanksgiving dinner, because I had to help out.

129
00:10:43.755 --> 00:10:57.208
So when we got a bigger house that was nice, she would always try to give me my own space, my own room.

130
00:10:57.208 --> 00:11:06.566
And this room was huge to me then, with the closet and . . . It was just gorgeous.

131
00:11:06.566 --> 00:11:13.000
<v COOPER>Do you . . . Is that the one on Old Spanish Trail?

132
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.421
<v SIMS>No. I done forgot the name of the street.

133
00:11:16.421 --> 00:11:19.083
Like I said, I moved a lot.

134
00:11:19.083 --> 00:11:22.504
<v COOPER>And what else do you remember about the house?

135
00:11:22.504 --> 00:11:24.405
What was it like outside?

136
00:11:24.405 --> 00:11:25.926
Was there a porch?

137
00:11:25.926 --> 00:11:26.686
<v SIMS>No porch.

138
00:11:26.686 --> 00:11:28.587
Trees . . . And that's about all.

139
00:11:28.587 --> 00:11:32.388
And it was in the back yard of other houses.

140
00:11:32.388 --> 00:11:34.289
<v COOPER>Did you have a garden?

141
00:11:34.289 --> 00:11:34.669
<v SIMS>No.

142
00:11:34.669 --> 00:11:41.512
<v COOPER>So what else . . . You said you were the oldest and that you had to prepare the holiday festivities.

143
00:11:41.512 --> 00:11:45.314
What else did you have to do around the house?

144
00:11:45.314 --> 00:11:50.256
<v SIMS>Make sure that all the jobs was done that my mom told me,

145
00:11:50.256 --> 00:11:57.099
and make sure that they . . . my sisters and my brothers obeyed me as would I was their mother.

146
00:11:57.099 --> 00:11:59.000
That was my duties.

147
00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.819
<v COOPER>And what sorts of jobs did you have to finish?

148
00:12:03.819 --> 00:12:15.867
<v SIMS>I had to cook, I had to wash, hang the clothes out, wash the dishes, make the beds, just the whole run of the house.

149
00:12:15.867 --> 00:12:23.578
I was my mom's baby sister, and she was my friend, and I was her friend.

150
00:12:23.578 --> 00:12:27.434
<v COOPER>When did you first consider yourself grown up?

151
00:12:27.434 --> 00:12:29.361
<v SIMS>I guess about twelve.

152
00:12:29.361 --> 00:12:31.771
<v COOPER>When you moved to Mossville.

153
00:12:31.771 --> 00:12:32.253
<v SIMS>[Agrees]

154
00:12:32.253 --> 00:12:39.000
<v COOPER>And what kind of values do you think your mother instilled in you?

155
00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:42.606
<v SIMS>To be respectful, to demand respect.

156
00:12:42.606 --> 00:12:46.812
People will only respect you if you

157
00:12:46.812 --> 00:12:58.230
<c.p>respect yourself, and don't let them cross that line because you have the final say how people treat you.

158
00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:03.638
Always listen to that little voice inside of you,

159
00:13:03.638 --> 00:13:22.869
because that is the spirit of God that keeps you grounded and keeps you going through the tough times and the times where you don't know how you're going to make it.

160
00:13:22.869 --> 00:13:26.474
To rely on your own knowledge,

161
00:13:26.474 --> 00:13:39.094
and in getting that knowledge you will get wisdom to focus on the things to make the world a better place.

162
00:13:39.094 --> 00:13:40.897
<v COOPER>That's really beautiful.

163
00:13:40.897 --> 00:13:49.310
Who . . . Okay, so who were the most important people to you at that time?

164
00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:58.925
<v SIMS>The most important people to me were people that were fathers and mothers that had children.

165
00:13:58.925 --> 00:14:02.531
That I saw as a family.

166
00:14:02.531 --> 00:14:09.141
That had nice jobs and did good things with their money.

167
00:14:09.141 --> 00:14:18.155
Teachers that instill the importance of your mind, that education is the thing to get,

168
00:14:18.155 --> 00:14:27.770
because when you get that it would afford you a better living and a better life.

169
00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:34.981
<v COOPER>And you were going to Mossville Elementary . . . What high school did you . . .

170
00:14:34.981 --> 00:14:39.789
<v SIMS>Oh, it was called . . . it was Mossville High.

171
00:14:39.789 --> 00:14:40.390
<v COOPER>Okay.

172
00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:41.592
Alright, yes.

173
00:14:41.592 --> 00:14:47.000
<v SIMS>But I went there my seventh, eighth grade.

174
00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.230
<v COOPER>I see.

175
00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:54.378
And do you remember any of the teachers from then?

176
00:14:54.378 --> 00:14:58.067
<v SIMS>My teachers were mentors to me.

177
00:14:58.067 --> 00:15:07.904
They saw something in me that I didn't even know was in me at the time.

178
00:15:07.904 --> 00:15:19.585
They let me know that I was smart and capable of doing anything I wanted to become and do.

179
00:15:19.585 --> 00:15:26.963
The most inspirational person to me was Ms. Rogers, my English teacher.

180
00:15:26.963 --> 00:15:40.489
The things she taught me is what I'm using now as a gift to being able to write, being able to speak,

181
00:15:40.489 --> 00:15:54.015
because we had to do a book report every week and stand up in front of the students and tell our story.

182
00:15:54.015 --> 00:16:10.000
That gave me the confidence and the know-how that was developed in doing this to stand before people and be able to speak with confidence.

183
00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:15.419
The next person was Mrs. Jones, Mary [Jane] Jones.

184
00:16:15.419 --> 00:16:18.429
She was my homeroom teacher.

185
00:16:18.429 --> 00:16:29.869
She would take us to Vinton [Louisiana], this town that she lived in with her husband and her son,

186
00:16:29.869 --> 00:16:38.901
and she showed me what a wife, a loving mother with a husband was about.

187
00:16:38.901 --> 00:16:42.513
So I pulled that from her.

188
00:16:42.513 --> 00:16:49.738
And the next person was Ms. Avery Prater, my home economics teacher.

189
00:16:49.738 --> 00:16:55.157
I was chosen my senior year to be her.

190
00:16:55.157 --> 00:17:04.188
We had a step up day, and everybody in the twelfth grade became a teacher.

191
00:17:04.188 --> 00:17:12.618
I was chosen to be her that day, which was an honor to me.

192
00:17:12.618 --> 00:17:21.649
But the things you see me do, the credibility that she brought out in me,

193
00:17:21.649 --> 00:17:36.702
I have just finished making . . . doing a pair of shoes that was ruined—the . . . whatever the material came off—and I put sequins on them and glitter.

194
00:17:36.702 --> 00:17:44.529
A purse that was coming loose, I learned how to put it together.

195
00:17:44.529 --> 00:17:55.969
The sewing I do, I do because I love to do it, not because I have to do it.

196
00:17:55.969 --> 00:18:05.000
So those things came out of me because of what she saw in me.

197
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.783
<v COOPER>And what year did you graduate from Mossville?

198
00:18:07.783 --> 00:18:08.130
<v SIMS>'65.

199
00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:08.478
<v COOPER>'65.

200
00:18:08.478 --> 00:18:08.826
<v SIMS>1965.

201
00:18:08.826 --> 00:18:09.870
<v COOPER>From Mossville High.

202
00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:13.000
What did you like and dislike about school?

203
00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:15.762
<v SIMS>I loved everything about school.

204
00:18:15.762 --> 00:18:18.525
School was my safe haven.

205
00:18:18.525 --> 00:18:21.840
There, I was who I was.

206
00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:25.707
I wasn't famous, but I was popular.

207
00:18:25.707 --> 00:18:32.336
I started dating my husband when I was in the ninth grade.

208
00:18:32.336 --> 00:18:35.099
I was thirteen years old.

209
00:18:35.099 --> 00:18:37.861
He was from Lake Charles.

210
00:18:37.861 --> 00:18:40.623
He was older than me.

211
00:18:40.623 --> 00:18:52.778
So I had a long term relationship with him for five years before we married, because he went off to Grambling College.

212
00:18:52.778 --> 00:19:01.617
He was my only boyfriend, the only man I ever knew, because that was my soulmate.

213
00:19:01.617 --> 00:19:13.772
And so even though I had a relationship with him, I was still popular with the boys because I stood my ground,

214
00:19:13.772 --> 00:19:20.401
I made them respect me, and they knew that I loved Roy.

215
00:19:20.401 --> 00:19:30.346
The ones that didn't respect that learned to respect it because that was what I made them do.

216
00:19:30.346 --> 00:19:34.213
I was a majorette for five years.

217
00:19:34.213 --> 00:19:42.500
I twirled with fire my junior year, which is something that everybody know me for.

218
00:19:42.500 --> 00:19:50.235
The young kids, when I tell them who I am and they don't remember,

219
00:19:50.235 --> 00:19:57.969
I'm like, "You heard about the girl who did the twirling with the fire?"

220
00:19:57.969 --> 00:20:00.179
"Oh yeah, Audrey Sims!"

221
00:20:00.179 --> 00:20:01.284
"That's me."

222
00:20:01.284 --> 00:20:05.704
My senior year was very rewarding to me.

223
00:20:05.704 --> 00:20:19.515
I went to Mossville with a group of many, but to be able to graduate as the only girl from that group was an honor.

224
00:20:19.515 --> 00:20:22.830
I finished in the top ten.

225
00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:26.698
I received the I Dare You Award,

226
00:20:26.698 --> 00:20:37.747
which was . . . for me to dare challenges that they saw in me that I didn't know I had in me.

227
00:20:37.747 --> 00:20:41.062
I received the American Legion Award.

228
00:20:41.062 --> 00:20:52.664
So there was a lot of things that the community of Mossville saw in me and allowed me to be me,

229
00:20:52.664 --> 00:20:56.531
for me to be who I am

230
00:20:56.531 --> 00:20:57.083
<c.p>today.

231
00:20:57.083 --> 00:21:02.056
<v COOPER>And did Roy also go to Mossville High School?

232
00:21:02.056 --> 00:21:02.608
<v SIMS>No.

233
00:21:02.608 --> 00:21:03.160
<v COOPER>No?

234
00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:06.475
<v SIMS>Roy went to Washington High School.

235
00:21:06.475 --> 00:21:09.238
<v COOPER>How did you meet him?

236
00:21:09.238 --> 00:21:10.895
<v SIMS>At a canteen . . .

237
00:21:10.895 --> 00:21:12.000
<v COOPER>Oh.

238
00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:12.585
<v SIMS>. . . dancing.

239
00:21:12.585 --> 00:21:19.600
Like I said, I was a majorette and I loved to dance.

240
00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:26.615
My father was a zydeco musician, and he played the rubbing board.

241
00:21:26.615 --> 00:21:29.538
So I grew up dancing.

242
00:21:29.538 --> 00:21:32.462
I grew up with music.

243
00:21:32.462 --> 00:21:35.969
It was just in my DNA.

244
00:21:35.969 --> 00:21:50.000
My mother's side of the family were also great dancers and lovers of music and fun loving people in good ways . . . good positive ways.

245
00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:56.431
So that setting, I had all of this talent in me.

246
00:21:56.431 --> 00:22:13.385
People accepted my talent and . . . One of the things I want to say about my instructors also—they saw in me things that made me happy being who I was.

247
00:22:13.385 --> 00:22:25.077
They never let the money I didn't have hinder me because someone would always give me the money for that

248
00:22:25.077 --> 00:22:27.415
<c.p>costume, for that trip.

249
00:22:27.415 --> 00:22:33.846
We went to American Bandstand when it was in Beaumont, Texas.

250
00:22:33.846 --> 00:22:44.954
I don't know who paid my way, but I went knowing that I didn't have the money to go.

251
00:22:44.954 --> 00:22:55.477
So like I said, if there was a costume that I had to have to be in something,

252
00:22:55.477 --> 00:23:02.492
someone would come and get the material for my mom to sew.

253
00:23:02.492 --> 00:23:06.000
She was a great seamstress, also.

254
00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.923
My mom was self-taught, self-made.

255
00:23:08.923 --> 00:23:18.862
To me, she was one of the most talented women I knew with a fourth grade education.

256
00:23:18.862 --> 00:23:20.615
She could read.

257
00:23:20.615 --> 00:23:22.369
She would write.

258
00:23:22.369 --> 00:23:26.462
She would make something out of nothing.

259
00:23:26.462 --> 00:23:38.154
She's noted for being one of the best pie makers that our family knew in the bunch of nine girls.

260
00:23:38.154 --> 00:23:42.246
I just had a fantastic, blessed life.

261
00:23:42.246 --> 00:23:44.000
I did.

262
00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:45.811
It was rough.

263
00:23:45.811 --> 00:23:56.075
There was a time in my life when . . . I don't share this with a lot of people,

264
00:23:56.075 --> 00:24:03.321
but I think now is the time for me to share it.

265
00:24:03.321 --> 00:24:11.170
My mother had married, and the man she married had gone to prison.

266
00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:15.396
And when . . . It was hard for us.

267
00:24:15.396 --> 00:24:20.226
When he came back, he couldn't find work.

268
00:24:20.226 --> 00:24:22.642
I was a senior.

269
00:24:22.642 --> 00:24:37.132
And he told Mom that he wanted us to leave and go to Texas for him to find a job to start all over.

270
00:24:37.132 --> 00:24:46.189
That became a burden to me, because I'm a senior, I'm popular, everybody know me.

271
00:24:46.189 --> 00:24:52.830
This is my life, so what am I going to do?

272
00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:56.453
Where am I going to be?

273
00:24:56.453 --> 00:25:00.075
And my grades start to suffer.

274
00:25:00.075 --> 00:25:08.528
Mrs. Rogers saw this and she asked, "Audrey Sims, what's going on with you?"

275
00:25:08.528 --> 00:25:11.547
And I said, "I'm afraid."

276
00:25:11.547 --> 00:25:16.377
And she said, "What are you afraid of?"

277
00:25:16.377 --> 00:25:29.660
And she and I in that little small place, I confided in her and she listened and she gave me good advice,

278
00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:35.698
because in my mind I didn't want a new beginning.

279
00:25:35.698 --> 00:25:42.943
I just wanted to finish the things I had accomplished with dignity.

280
00:25:42.943 --> 00:25:47.170
And to lose that was very disturbing.

281
00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:52.604
But she said, "Don't look at the bad side.

282
00:25:52.604 --> 00:25:55.019
God holds your future.

283
00:25:55.019 --> 00:26:05.887
Just continue to do the things you've been doing and pray about it, because God knows your destiny.

284
00:26:05.887 --> 00:26:13.736
And in my heart I don't feel you have to worry about anything.”

285
00:26:13.736 --> 00:26:18.566
And that passed and we didn't go anywhere.

286
00:26:18.566 --> 00:26:24.000
I finished school and I'm proud about it.

287
00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:24.480
<v COOPER>Wow.

288
00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:30.245
That sounds like a really incredible relationship you had with Ms. Rogers.

289
00:26:30.245 --> 00:26:36.490
How did school compare . . . How was it different before you came to Mossville?

290
00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:40.814
<v SIMS>I was at schools that were of great volume.

291
00:26:40.814 --> 00:26:45.137
There, you was just a name and a number.

292
00:26:45.137 --> 00:26:57.147
Although I was smart and people would pick me out of the group, because when I was in third grade, because of my dancing abilities,

293
00:26:57.147 --> 00:27:09.637
I was chosen to do dancing and the high school girls would teach us, and there was a musical called Cherry Pink and Apple Blossom White.

294
00:27:09.637 --> 00:27:14.922
I was Apple Blossom White, and the girl was Cherry Pink.

295
00:27:14.922 --> 00:27:21.167
I met her for the first time in fifty years this past October.

296
00:27:21.167 --> 00:27:27.892
That I thought she was gone, but she was there and she remembered me.

297
00:27:27.892 --> 00:27:34.137
So even in the third grade I was chosen to be a majorette.

298
00:27:34.137 --> 00:27:39.422
Out of all the girls at Washington Elementary, I was chosen.

299
00:27:39.422 --> 00:27:41.824
So I did have talent.

300
00:27:41.824 --> 00:27:47.108
Somebody saw something in me that I didn't know I had.

301
00:27:47.108 --> 00:27:53.353
<v COOPER>And what was it like doing . . . being a majorette at Mossville High School?

302
00:27:53.353 --> 00:28:02.000
Do you remember who your coach was, and maybe some other people who did it with you?

303
00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:06.323
<v SIMS>I remember the coach was a talented lady.

304
00:28:06.323 --> 00:28:10.646
She was there my first and second year.

305
00:28:10.646 --> 00:28:19.292
And then the band instructor, because the coach of the majorette squad was the P.E. teacher.

306
00:28:19.292 --> 00:28:32.262
So when she left, the next P.E. teacher that took her place wasn't into that; she was more of the sporty, basketball, baseball coach.

307
00:28:32.262 --> 00:28:36.045
So the band director became our mentor.

308
00:28:36.045 --> 00:28:43.070
When I got to the tenth grade, we would pass the baton on.

309
00:28:43.070 --> 00:28:52.797
It was known that I would get to be the next head majorette twirling because of my abilities.

310
00:28:52.797 --> 00:29:06.847
And that was an honor because here I'm an immigrant just coming in, but they accept me as one of the best in that family community.

311
00:29:06.847 --> 00:29:09.008
So that was an

312
00:29:09.008 --> 00:29:10.630
<c.p>honor for me.

313
00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:14.953
And I did it with pride and honor.

314
00:29:14.953 --> 00:29:16.574
I stepped high.

315
00:29:16.574 --> 00:29:19.276
I still can twirl today.

316
00:29:19.276 --> 00:29:31.164
I still have my twirling, and I did it . . . We had our fiftieth high school reunion this past May and I twirled.

317
00:29:31.164 --> 00:29:32.245
<v COOPER>With fire?

318
00:29:32.245 --> 00:29:40.351
<v SIMS>Not with the fire [laughs], no. But I did it because it's still in me.

319
00:29:40.351 --> 00:29:50.618
<v COOPER>Did you ever get to travel outside of Mossville being a majorette, or would you go to other schools?

320
00:29:50.618 --> 00:29:58.184
<v SIMS>Oh, we went . . . Being a majorette, we went with the best . . . the football team.

321
00:29:58.184 --> 00:30:01.967
So wherever they went, we were there.

322
00:30:01.967 --> 00:30:07.911
We made a name for having a great band, great majorettes.

323
00:30:07.911 --> 00:30:18.178
The biggest joy I got out of being proud to be a majorette, when we went for Mardi Gras.

324
00:30:18.178 --> 00:30:29.526
We didn't have Mardi Gras in Lake Charles back in the day when I was a young girl; it happened afterwards.

325
00:30:29.526 --> 00:30:39.794
But we would go to Lafayette [Louisiana] and there was another band director that was over Washington High School,

326
00:30:39.794 --> 00:30:44.117
so he was like part time with us.

327
00:30:44.117 --> 00:30:46.819
He had the two schools.

328
00:30:46.819 --> 00:30:56.546
They would put us in front of the big band . . . the little country school with the big band.

329
00:30:56.546 --> 00:31:01.409
But when we passed, everybody would cheer and say,

330
00:31:01.409 --> 00:31:11.136
"Look at those corn-fed girls!" [laughter] We had big legs, we had good rhythm, and we were proud.

331
00:31:11.136 --> 00:31:16.000
I was proud to represent Mossville High School.

332
00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:20.606
<v COOPER>What else did you do at Mossville High School?

333
00:31:20.606 --> 00:31:24.189
Were you involved in any other extracurriculars?

334
00:31:24.189 --> 00:31:25.724
<v SIMS>I played basketball.

335
00:31:25.724 --> 00:31:30.843
I tried to run track, but track wasn't my thing.

336
00:31:30.843 --> 00:31:32.378
I played tennis.

337
00:31:32.378 --> 00:31:35.961
I did the choir a little bit.

338
00:31:35.961 --> 00:31:40.055
I was vice president of the student counsel.

339
00:31:40.055 --> 00:31:51.827
I was in a lot of political things in school, leaderships in school, because that was my area; that was my safe haven.

340
00:31:51.827 --> 00:31:54.898
School was away from the problems.

341
00:31:54.898 --> 00:32:00.016
It was just a happy place for me to be.

342
00:32:00.016 --> 00:32:00.528
<v COOPER>Okay.

343
00:32:00.528 --> 00:32:07.693
There's so many I want . . . so many things I want to ask you about.

344
00:32:07.693 --> 00:32:15.370
But I think first, you mentioned that you met your future husband at the canteen.

345
00:32:15.370 --> 00:32:21.000
Could you explain more of what the canteen was like?

346
00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:30.946
<v SIMS>During the '60s . . . late '50s and '60s, we had a sock hop that we called canteens.

347
00:32:30.946 --> 00:32:42.757
That's where the teenagers would get together, drink coke, and dance to the Temptations, all the famous R&amp;B talents.

348
00:32:42.757 --> 00:32:55.811
I was a dancer and I stayed on the floor from the time I'd get there until the time I'd leave,

349
00:32:55.811 --> 00:33:03.892
dancing with boys that wanted to dance because I was a good dancer.

350
00:33:03.892 --> 00:33:07.622
My husband was not a dancer.

351
00:33:07.622 --> 00:33:11.973
He was back in the corner watching.

352
00:33:11.973 --> 00:33:21.297
And when it got time for us to leave . . . I lived right around the block,

353
00:33:21.297 --> 00:33:37.459
and that was one of my gifts that my mom gave to me to let me come to Lake Charles to be with my daddy's people.

354
00:33:37.459 --> 00:33:45.541
And he walked me . . . he asked to walk me home and I agreed.

355
00:33:45.541 --> 00:34:00.459
When we got to my aunt's house—that was my mom's sister; she lived down the alley, we called it—he asked could he kiss me.

356
00:34:00.459 --> 00:34:09.162
I told him I didn't kiss a guy the first time I met him.

357
00:34:09.162 --> 00:34:17.243
He reminded me that that was not our first time meeting each other.

358
00:34:17.243 --> 00:34:21.595
He had been going with my cousin.

359
00:34:21.595 --> 00:34:25.946
And I said, "Well that doesn't count."

360
00:34:25.946 --> 00:34:33.405
He reached down and he kissed me and he said, "Now what?"

361
00:34:33.405 --> 00:34:38.378
And I embraced him and we kissed again.

362
00:34:38.378 --> 00:34:47.081
That was December seventeenth, 1961, because after we graduated . . . I graduated and he graduated . . .

363
00:34:47.081 --> 00:34:58.892
<c.p>before he graduated from college his last year, I married him the day before my birthday, my nineteenth birthday.

364
00:34:58.892 --> 00:35:11.946
And I asked God if he would let me have him as a husband for fifty years, I would be happy.

365
00:35:11.946 --> 00:35:23.757
God gave me that; because he died on my birthday fifty years, two months, my anniversary and my birthday.

366
00:35:23.757 --> 00:35:26.865
So God gave me favor.

367
00:35:26.865 --> 00:35:27.486
<v COOPER>Wow.

368
00:35:27.486 --> 00:35:40.541
<v SIMS>And he died in Mississippi, because that's where he wanted to take me for our anniversary, forty-five years, and my birthday.

369
00:35:40.541 --> 00:35:42.405
I was sixty-four.

370
00:35:42.405 --> 00:35:48.000
So that was a great gift to me.

371
00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:48.436
<v COOPER>Wow.

372
00:35:48.436 --> 00:35:52.362
So that was at the canteen in Lake Charles.

373
00:35:52.362 --> 00:35:57.597
What did . . . Did you ever go to the canteen, like Ms. Lula's?

374
00:35:57.597 --> 00:36:00.651
Some people talk about Ms. Lula's canteen.

375
00:36:00.651 --> 00:36:05.450
<v SIMS>I'm sure I did, but it was . . . just to go there.

376
00:36:05.450 --> 00:36:08.503
This canteen was a landmark for me.

377
00:36:08.503 --> 00:36:11.557
It's still there, not a canteen anymore.

378
00:36:11.557 --> 00:36:14.174
It's been a lot of things.

379
00:36:14.174 --> 00:36:18.973
But it's still right there in Lake Charles on Opelousas Street.

380
00:36:18.973 --> 00:36:24.644
And I'll show my children, I'll show my grandkids where it all begun.

381
00:36:24.644 --> 00:36:29.879
<v COOPER>What else would you do for entertainment when you were that age?

382
00:36:29.879 --> 00:36:31.624
<v SIMS>Dancing was my life.

383
00:36:31.624 --> 00:36:35.114
I ate, slept, I choreographed the majorette steps.

384
00:36:35.114 --> 00:36:43.403
I'd hear a record and it would just be in me and I could move and make up steps,

385
00:36:43.403 --> 00:36:47.329
and bring it back to school and teach them.

386
00:36:47.329 --> 00:36:49.074
That's what I did.

387
00:36:49.074 --> 00:36:53.000
<v COOPER>What sort of music did you listen to?

388
00:36:53.000 --> 00:37:00.850
<v SIMS>Like I said, in the '60s it was . . . we call it "old school" now.

389
00:37:00.850 --> 00:37:05.897
But it was rhythm and blues, rock and roll.

390
00:37:05.897 --> 00:37:10.944
<v COOPER>And would you ever get to hear live music?

391
00:37:10.944 --> 00:37:13.748
<v SIMS>No. I take that back.

392
00:37:13.748 --> 00:37:26.645
There was a band in Westlake and sometimes he would play when they would have . . . Oh, I was the seventh grade queen [laughs].

393
00:37:26.645 --> 00:37:36.178
There again, it's unique because I'm an outsider and here they elect me to be the queen.

394
00:37:36.178 --> 00:37:39.542
They had the live band there.

395
00:37:39.542 --> 00:37:42.907
<v COOPER>And where was the celebration held?

396
00:37:42.907 --> 00:37:48.514
<v SIMS>It was in Westlake at the cafeteria . . . in the cafeteria.

397
00:37:48.514 --> 00:37:53.000
<v COOPER>Did you ever go to the movies?

398
00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:56.971
<v SIMS>Only when Roy would come home. [laughs]

399
00:37:56.971 --> 00:37:58.106
<v COOPER>Let's see.

400
00:37:58.106 --> 00:38:03.780
Okay, you also mentioned that your mother was a seamstress.

401
00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:10.020
Did she sew clothes for other people in the neighborhood too?

402
00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:17.963
<v SIMS>My mom could look at anything and create it out of brown paper bags.

403
00:38:17.963 --> 00:38:19.098
After Roy

404
00:38:19.098 --> 00:38:29.878
<c.p>and I had married, my husband was an Alpha in a fraternity and they were having the convention here.

405
00:38:29.878 --> 00:38:32.714
I wanted to be special.

406
00:38:32.714 --> 00:38:46.331
Money was hard for he and I. I think I was working at St. Pat Hospital during that time and he was a teacher.

407
00:38:46.331 --> 00:38:51.437
But I wanted to make him proud of me.

408
00:38:51.437 --> 00:39:04.486
So while I was there I was looking in a magazine and I saw Jacqueline Kennedy gown that she wore for the inauguration.

409
00:39:04.486 --> 00:39:14.131
I tore it out, brought it to my mom, and I said, "Mom, can you make this?"

410
00:39:14.131 --> 00:39:16.400
And she said, "Yeah."

411
00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:19.804
She knew exactly how much material.

412
00:39:19.804 --> 00:39:26.045
We did it in black crepe with gold sequins, ribbon around.

413
00:39:26.045 --> 00:39:32.286
It was one shoulder out with a cape attached to it.

414
00:39:32.286 --> 00:39:36.257
My mom made that dress, that gown.

415
00:39:36.257 --> 00:39:40.229
I was the talk of the ball.

416
00:39:40.229 --> 00:39:47.037
All the men was asking where they could buy that dress from.

417
00:39:47.037 --> 00:39:55.547
And when I informed them that my mother had made it, they wanted the pattern,

418
00:39:55.547 --> 00:40:04.057
and I said, "Oh no." That dress came out in Vogue patterns five years later.

419
00:40:04.057 --> 00:40:12.000
But I wore it the same year of the inauguration of the president.

420
00:40:12.000 --> 00:40:17.750
<v COOPER>Where would your mom get fabrics from?

421
00:40:17.750 --> 00:40:20.214
Do you remember?

422
00:40:20.214 --> 00:40:25.143
<v SIMS>[People?], curtains, old clothes taken apart.

423
00:40:25.143 --> 00:40:28.429
I could do that.

424
00:40:28.429 --> 00:40:36.643
And I do it gracefully, because that's what I know.

425
00:40:36.643 --> 00:40:42.393
<v COOPER>So you learned that from your mother?

426
00:40:42.393 --> 00:40:43.214
<v SIMS>Yes.

427
00:40:43.214 --> 00:40:52.250
<v COOPER>What other skills do you think you learned from your mother?

428
00:40:52.250 --> 00:40:58.000
<v SIMS>Cooking, organizing, management, thriftiness, clean house.

429
00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:03.028
<v COOPER>What sorts of meals did she teach you to cook?

430
00:41:03.028 --> 00:41:06.045
<v SIMS>You name it, I cook it.

431
00:41:06.045 --> 00:41:10.067
I thank God for memory, for my mind.

432
00:41:10.067 --> 00:41:18.112
The first time I cooked by myself with no supervision because I watched, and I learned,

433
00:41:18.112 --> 00:41:27.665
and I wanted to please my mom, I was eight years old and I was cooking some white beans.

434
00:41:27.665 --> 00:41:33.698
I cooked them all day long and they never would get soft.

435
00:41:33.698 --> 00:41:41.743
I just . . . all I know she did was boil them, but I know they was soft.

436
00:41:41.743 --> 00:41:53.810
So when my momma got off by four o'clock I said, "Momma, I tried to cook some beans, but they never would get soft."

437
00:41:53.810 --> 00:41:57.330
She said, "Tell me what you did."

438
00:41:57.330 --> 00:42:06.380
I said, "I washed them, I soaked them, I boiled them, I put . . .” We had some salt meat,

439
00:42:06.380 --> 00:42:12.916
the little that we did have, maybe a piece of sausage—I don't know.

440
00:42:12.916 --> 00:42:15.933
And I put salt and pepper.

441
00:42:15.933 --> 00:42:26.994
And she looked at me and she smiled and she said, "You didn't see me put the oil in it, did you?"

442
00:42:26.994 --> 00:42:28.000
<v COOPER>Oh.

443
00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:28.991
<v SIMS>"No ma'am."

444
00:42:28.991 --> 00:42:36.916
And to this day I know beans do not get soft unless you add the oil.

445
00:42:36.916 --> 00:42:40.383
So that was a lesson well learned.

446
00:42:40.383 --> 00:42:51.280
<v COOPER>Are there any meals now that you . . . are served for you or that you cook that bring you back to your childhood?

447
00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:57.224
<v SIMS>You may think this funny, but my best wildlife meat is coon.

448
00:42:57.224 --> 00:42:59.701
So when I get some

449
00:42:59.701 --> 00:43:11.589
<c.p>coon that somebody cooks that they don't ever cook anymore, it brings me back to my mom because that was a delicacy for us.

450
00:43:11.589 --> 00:43:14.561
And boy could she cook it.

451
00:43:14.561 --> 00:43:18.523
<v COOPER>What else did she cook that you remember?

452
00:43:18.523 --> 00:43:21.000
What were your favorites?

453
00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:24.023
<v SIMS>She did tripe, black-eyed peas, biscuits.

454
00:43:24.023 --> 00:43:28.557
She's noted for her lemon pies and potato pies.

455
00:43:28.557 --> 00:43:34.099
I loved her syrup cake that she would do every weekend.

456
00:43:34.099 --> 00:43:46.191
When I would get off from work during beauty school, I'd go visit her and have a slice of syrup cake with some coffee.

457
00:43:46.191 --> 00:43:48.710
She was a great cook.

458
00:43:48.710 --> 00:43:57.275
There's nothing I could say that Mom cooked that I didn't like, and she cooked it all.

459
00:43:57.275 --> 00:44:04.832
Like I said, she made a meal . . . Another story when we were really doing bad,

460
00:44:04.832 --> 00:44:09.366
really, really, really know what having without really meant.

461
00:44:09.366 --> 00:44:24.985
When we had to go to Rayne [Louisiana] after Mom and my dad separated and she lived with her sister and then finally we got a little house of our own,

462
00:44:24.985 --> 00:44:36.573
she was working in a sweet potato field, digging sweet potatoes for a living for us, which was maybe five dollars a day.

463
00:44:36.573 --> 00:44:42.618
We had sweet potatoes any way you could name it being cooked.

464
00:44:42.618 --> 00:44:54.710
Like the man that made the sweet potato that did that, we had any kind of meal you can make from a sweet potato.

465
00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:56.725
That kept us going.

466
00:44:56.725 --> 00:45:03.779
And there we had a garden, because she dug in the ground by herself.

467
00:45:03.779 --> 00:45:06.802
Maybe I helped; I don't remember.

468
00:45:06.802 --> 00:45:16.878
But I can see the rows, I can see the mustard greens, I can see the turnips, the bell peppers.

469
00:45:16.878 --> 00:45:33.000
The little things that she couldn't afford to go to the store to buy she took upon herself to do the labor of planting that we could have that at least.

470
00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:39.435
<v COOPER>Did she ever grow any plants for home remedies or for medicine?

471
00:45:39.435 --> 00:45:46.406
<v SIMS>No. We just knew where they were and what they were used for.

472
00:45:46.406 --> 00:45:51.232
<v COOPER>And where would she do her grocery shopping at?

473
00:45:51.232 --> 00:45:53.377
<v SIMS>At the family grocery.

474
00:45:53.377 --> 00:46:00.348
We never . . . When I was coming up, we never went to big stores.

475
00:46:00.348 --> 00:46:03.565
We just went to community stores.

476
00:46:03.565 --> 00:46:10.000
<v COOPER>Do you remember where in the community the grocery store was?

477
00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:13.121
<v SIMS>Yes, on Sulphur Avenue in Westlake.

478
00:46:13.121 --> 00:46:20.405
There's that same stretch going to Mossville, because we lived in, like I said,

479
00:46:20.405 --> 00:46:29.769
Westlake first and that's where we migrated from and we moved to Mossville when my stepfather came back.

480
00:46:29.769 --> 00:46:33.931
But yes, that's where we made our groceries.

481
00:46:33.931 --> 00:46:39.653
I remember the first time I spent money in the store.

482
00:46:39.653 --> 00:46:46.416
I loved my mom so much, and I'm just a lover I imagine.

483
00:46:46.416 --> 00:46:49.017
And I like to give.

484
00:46:49.017 --> 00:46:52.139
I like to show my love.

485
00:46:52.139 --> 00:46:56.301
My mom's birthday was Saturday, on the fifth.

486
00:46:56.301 --> 00:47:04.104
I wanted to give her something for her birthday, because nobody would give her anything.

487
00:47:04.104 --> 00:47:15.029
So I saved . . . I don't know if it was fifty cents or twenty-five cents, a chore that I did for somebody.

488
00:47:15.029 --> 00:47:17.630
She liked Baby Ruth candy.

489
00:47:17.630 --> 00:47:25.954
I bought her a Baby Ruth candy and a white bowl for cereal for her birthday.

490
00:47:25.954 --> 00:47:31.676
That was my first gift to her with my own money.

491
00:47:31.676 --> 00:47:35.318
<v COOPER>And where did you purchase it from?

492
00:47:35.318 --> 00:47:40.000
<v SIMS>From the grocery, the neighborhood grocery store [laughs].

493
00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:45.867
<v COOPER>And where would you go to buy clothing and other stuff?

494
00:47:45.867 --> 00:47:47.467
<v SIMS>I had hand-me-downs.

495
00:47:47.467 --> 00:47:59.200
The lady that my mom worked for, her grandchildren or nieces, whatever they didn't want they would give them to my mom.

496
00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:10.933
We'd take them apart because they were like the poodle skirts, and sit there and make the newest fashions out of them.

497
00:48:10.933 --> 00:48:16.800
I was cool, because when I wore them they was fashionable.

498
00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:22.133
And that's what I do now; I dress that way.

499
00:48:22.133 --> 00:48:32.267
Every day I dress fashionable because I like clothes, and I like looking pretty, and I like feeling good.

500
00:48:32.267 --> 00:48:37.600
<v COOPER>So you mentioned that you worked at St. Pat Hospital.

501
00:48:37.600 --> 00:48:40.267
Was that your first job?

502
00:48:40.267 --> 00:48:40.800
<v SIMS>No.

503
00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:44.000
<v COOPER>What was your first job?

504
00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:49.189
<v SIMS>My first job was taking care . . . Let me go back.

505
00:48:49.189 --> 00:48:54.379
My first job, very, very first job, was cleaning houses,

506
00:48:54.379 --> 00:49:05.795
which my mom would be furious because she said that was a hard life and she didn't see that life for me,

507
00:49:05.795 --> 00:49:13.580
because she knew I was smarter than that and I could do better than her.

508
00:49:13.580 --> 00:49:25.515
So now I understand why every generation wants their children to be better and do better, because of the hard roads they had.

509
00:49:25.515 --> 00:49:30.705
So my very first job was cleaning houses and babysitting.

510
00:49:30.705 --> 00:49:34.856
And I tried to keep it from her.

511
00:49:34.856 --> 00:49:47.830
One of my classmates got me a job taking care of this young girl that had a baby and she was working in a restaurant.

512
00:49:47.830 --> 00:50:02.360
The deal was she would give me two dollars a day to watch her baby and clean her house for food that she was going to give me

513
00:50:02.360 --> 00:50:05.473
<c.p>for lunch and the two dollars.

514
00:50:05.473 --> 00:50:10.144
When I went there the house was a mess.

515
00:50:10.144 --> 00:50:18.447
And I cleaned her house, took care of her baby, I made friends with the neighbors.

516
00:50:18.447 --> 00:50:27.269
But that evening my mom found out, and she came and got me and said, "No ma'am.

517
00:50:27.269 --> 00:50:29.864
You will not do this.

518
00:50:29.864 --> 00:50:38.686
I don't care what you need, what you think you can get, I will not allow this."

519
00:50:38.686 --> 00:50:40.242
And she wouldn't.

520
00:50:40.242 --> 00:50:45.432
And I didn't do anything until I finished high school.

521
00:50:45.432 --> 00:50:54.773
I came to live with my father in Lake Charles and I worked at the College Inn Cafe,

522
00:50:54.773 --> 00:51:01.000
across the street from McNeese, and I did classes at McNeese.

523
00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:04.422
<v COOPER>And what were you studying at McNeese?

524
00:51:04.422 --> 00:51:05.400
<v SIMS>P.E. [laughs].

525
00:51:05.400 --> 00:51:16.156
I wanted to be a P.E. teacher because of all of my . . . But I wound up teaching reading in the elementary school.

526
00:51:16.156 --> 00:51:18.111
<v COOPER>At which elementary school?

527
00:51:18.111 --> 00:51:19.089
<v SIMS>Eastwood Elementary.

528
00:51:19.089 --> 00:51:23.000
<v COOPER>For how long were you in that?

529
00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:23.845
<v SIMS>Seven years.

530
00:51:23.845 --> 00:51:24.689
<v COOPER>Seven years.

531
00:51:24.689 --> 00:51:27.646
And then you went back to school.

532
00:51:27.646 --> 00:51:28.913
Is that right?

533
00:51:28.913 --> 00:51:33.981
<v SIMS>I went . . . Yes, went back to school at SOWELA [Technical Community College]

534
00:51:33.981 --> 00:51:35.248
<c.p>in industrial technology.

535
00:51:35.248 --> 00:51:44.963
Worked at Olin within three months after I got that, because I wanted to make more money and I said to my husband,

536
00:51:44.963 --> 00:51:51.720
"Why don't you go and try to get a job across the lake at the plants?"

537
00:51:51.720 --> 00:51:56.366
And his answer was to me, "I like what I'm doing.

538
00:51:56.366 --> 00:51:57.634
I'm in education.

539
00:51:57.634 --> 00:52:02.702
If you want to make more money, you quit and you go."

540
00:52:02.702 --> 00:52:05.658
And in three months I was there.

541
00:52:05.658 --> 00:52:08.193
I made more money than him.

542
00:52:08.193 --> 00:52:10.727
That's where we got our start.

543
00:52:10.727 --> 00:52:17.484
And I worked there until the [President] Reagan administration when they [Olin] started laying everybody off.

544
00:52:17.484 --> 00:52:24.242
And from there I was off like three months and God came to me and said,

545
00:52:24.242 --> 00:52:31.000
"I want you to go and do what you always wanted to do, your passion."

546
00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:42.916
My passion was hair, because that was how I made my money in high school, pressing the neighborhood kids' hair and my classmates' hair.

547
00:52:42.916 --> 00:52:51.357
So when I went there the money that I got from the layoff paid for my education.

548
00:52:51.357 --> 00:53:01.287
And when I landed there the owner of the school saw my talent, saw my abilities and said to me,

549
00:53:01.287 --> 00:53:10.720
"If you promise to work for me a year, I will pay for your education to be an instructor."

550
00:53:10.720 --> 00:53:13.203
And that's how that happened.

551
00:53:13.203 --> 00:53:21.147
So I worked for her at Denham’s Beauty School, her and her son . . . her step son.

552
00:53:21.147 --> 00:53:33.063
From there I went to Sulphur and was an instructor for that beauty school, and I owned my own business for over twenty-five years.

553
00:53:33.063 --> 00:53:42.000
Because from the money I saved working at Olin, my husband and I built a beauty shop.

554
00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:46.397
<v COOPER>And what did you do at Olin?

555
00:53:46.397 --> 00:53:48.910
<v SIMS>I was an operator.

556
00:53:48.910 --> 00:53:49.538
<v COOPER>Alright.

557
00:53:49.538 --> 00:53:53.308
<v SIMS>There again, I was a first.

558
00:53:53.308 --> 00:54:00.218
We were divided into A, B, C, D operators and squads.

559
00:54:00.218 --> 00:54:03.987
I was in the B squad.

560
00:54:03.987 --> 00:54:14.038
Mr. Walker saw something in me again that he wanted me to be a lead operator.

561
00:54:14.038 --> 00:54:21.577
And I'm a woman, and I'm just this five foot tall woman.

562
00:54:21.577 --> 00:54:28.487
It was a competition between me, he, myself and another guy.

563
00:54:28.487 --> 00:54:31.000
Men will try you.

564
00:54:31.000 --> 00:54:33.513
Men will challenge you.

565
00:54:33.513 --> 00:54:36.654
You got to stand strong.

566
00:54:36.654 --> 00:54:41.679
But it began to be not a joy.

567
00:54:41.679 --> 00:54:46.077
It was going to be a battle.

568
00:54:46.077 --> 00:54:55.500
And I had two children at home and a husband, so I wouldn't take it.

569
00:54:55.500 --> 00:55:06.808
It wasn't worth it, because being at the top is not always what it's cracked up to be.

570
00:55:06.808 --> 00:55:13.718
With that, a great responsibility becomes a load and a burden.

571
00:55:13.718 --> 00:55:20.000
And I saw that and I didn't want that.

572
00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:24.759
<v COOPER>And when were you working at the hospital?

573
00:55:24.759 --> 00:55:28.924
<v SIMS>I started working at the hospital—another first.

574
00:55:28.924 --> 00:55:40.228
This is how God played an important part in my life when I didn't even know . . . I knew God,

575
00:55:40.228 --> 00:55:47.962
but I didn't know him at the level that I know him now.

576
00:55:47.962 --> 00:55:55.101
I went to the hospital being an escort to the x-ray department.

577
00:55:55.101 --> 00:56:07.000
But before then, I was a short order cook that had to serve the nuns their breakfast in the morning.

578
00:56:07.000 --> 00:56:11.165
There again, they saw something in me.

579
00:56:11.165 --> 00:56:25.443
Some of them began to be close to me because of my love and my compassion, and I didn't make difference with no one.

580
00:56:25.443 --> 00:56:39.127
So when I started having problems in my relationship with my husband, and I knew I had to stand for who I was,

581
00:56:39.127 --> 00:56:54.000
I went to one of them and told her I had a need to get a job that I could catch the bus to work,

582
00:56:54.000 --> 00:57:04.114
because the hours I had to be there was before six o'clock, and I had a baby.

583
00:57:04.114 --> 00:57:13.038
There she stepped in, talked to the doctors, got me in on the x-ray floor.

584
00:57:13.038 --> 00:57:16.608
The doctors saw something in me.

585
00:57:16.608 --> 00:57:26.127
Told me I was too smart to push people to the room, to the x-ray department.

586
00:57:26.127 --> 00:57:33.861
I became the first black x-ray technician student that he was paying for.

587
00:57:33.861 --> 00:57:35.646
I did great.

588
00:57:35.646 --> 00:57:39.215
<c.p>Nobody thought I would do it,

589
00:57:39.215 --> 00:57:54.089
and I was just shocking them because they had nuns and I was the only black and my grades was equal to all of them.

590
00:57:54.089 --> 00:58:04.203
Like I said, if you give me the books that I can obtain the knowledge like you,

591
00:58:04.203 --> 00:58:11.342
I can do better or at the ability where God have me.

592
00:58:11.342 --> 00:58:16.101
The misfortune of that was I got pregnant.

593
00:58:16.101 --> 00:58:19.671
I left because of the radiation.

594
00:58:19.671 --> 00:58:29.190
Although I lost the baby, but to see how God loves me and gave me favor.

595
00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:35.734
The young lady that was pregnant with me in x-ray stayed.

596
00:58:35.734 --> 00:58:41.684
Her baby was born retarded and lived for thirty-two years.

597
00:58:41.684 --> 00:58:48.823
And he knew my heart and he knew I couldn't do it.

598
00:58:48.823 --> 00:58:59.532
So for everything I go through in life, I choose to believe that it's God's plan for me.

599
00:58:59.532 --> 00:59:12.025
For me to be brave enough, willing enough to trust his judgement that it's all going to be for my betterment.

600
00:59:12.025 --> 00:59:15.000
And that's my life.

601
00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:21.423
<v COOPER>So you mentioned that you were the first black x-ray technician student.

602
00:59:21.423 --> 00:59:23.563
What was that like?

603
00:59:23.563 --> 00:59:29.986
Because that was just around the time of the Civil Rights movement.

604
00:59:29.986 --> 00:59:36.408
What was it like in Lake Charles and Mossville during that time?

605
00:59:36.408 --> 00:59:40.690
<v SIMS>It was an honor to represent your race,

606
00:59:40.690 --> 00:59:51.394
for people to know we had the intelligence to be able to function on any level if given the opportunity.

607
00:59:51.394 --> 00:59:59.423
I was fortunate enough to go on the boats because the doctor had this yacht,

608
00:59:59.423 --> 01:00:09.056
and I was on there with all of them seeing things that I thought I would never see.

609
01:00:09.056 --> 01:00:21.366
They had a convention, and the funny thing about it, the lady that took my place, her husband was a . . . in a band.

610
01:00:21.366 --> 01:00:30.465
So she was always attending these parties and whatever, and I realized I didn't have a lot.

611
01:00:30.465 --> 01:00:36.887
So she offered to let me use one of her party dresses.

612
01:00:36.887 --> 01:00:41.169
So Roy and myself going to the convention,

613
01:00:41.169 --> 01:00:51.873
and I walked in and I was like the bling of the party because they had on blue jeans [laughs].

614
01:00:51.873 --> 01:00:55.620
And here we are all dressed out.

615
01:00:55.620 --> 01:00:57.761
That was our culture!

616
01:00:57.761 --> 01:01:04.718
When we went to stuff like that, we dressed to kill—is the expression.

617
01:01:04.718 --> 01:01:07.930
So I felt out of place

618
01:01:07.930 --> 01:01:09.000
<c.p>[laughs].

619
01:01:09.000 --> 01:01:11.150
<v COOPER>That's so funny [laughs].

620
01:01:11.150 --> 01:01:13.838
<v SIMS>It was funny then [laughs].

621
01:01:13.838 --> 01:01:18.138
<v COOPER>So how was Lake Charles different than Mossville?

622
01:01:18.138 --> 01:01:28.888
Or was it different than Mossville in terms of segregation and what was going on with the Civil Rights movement?

623
01:01:28.888 --> 01:01:33.188
<v SIMS>In Mossville area it was a safe haven.

624
01:01:33.188 --> 01:01:36.950
You didn't think about race and color.

625
01:01:36.950 --> 01:01:39.100
You was all family.

626
01:01:39.100 --> 01:01:40.712
Everybody . . . same cloth.

627
01:01:40.712 --> 01:01:43.400
Everybody did the same thing.

628
01:01:43.400 --> 01:01:45.012
Everybody embraced you.

629
01:01:45.012 --> 01:01:56.838
It was nothing for my girlfriends, like Della Dotson and Nellie [Comeaux?], to give me something and I felt looked down on.

630
01:01:56.838 --> 01:01:59.525
That was just my friend.

631
01:01:59.525 --> 01:02:06.512
They was just sharing with me what they had that they didn't want.

632
01:02:06.512 --> 01:02:09.200
And I was very appreciative.

633
01:02:09.200 --> 01:02:19.412
My life in Lake Charles during the Civil Rights, like I said that was during the time of change.

634
01:02:19.412 --> 01:02:26.938
I already knew who I was, knew that I wanted the best for myself.

635
01:02:26.938 --> 01:02:30.700
Color didn't make a difference to me.

636
01:02:30.700 --> 01:02:31.238
<v COOPER>Uh-oh.

637
01:02:31.238 --> 01:02:32.312
Hold on.

638
01:02:32.312 --> 01:02:35.000
You're still connected [laughs].

639
01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:41.409
<v SIMS>I wanted to show you a picture, but I'll do it later.

640
01:02:41.409 --> 01:02:41.943
<v COOPER>Okay.

641
01:02:41.943 --> 01:02:51.557
Or we can . . . If you want to grab it right now, I can hold your microphone for you.

642
01:02:51.557 --> 01:02:53.693
Yes, let's do that.

643
01:02:53.693 --> 01:02:54.227
Alright.

644
01:02:54.227 --> 01:03:00.636
<v SIMS>Let's see if I can find it real quick. [break in tape]

645
01:03:00.636 --> 01:03:04.909
<v COOPER>Okay, so I'm going to start it again.

646
01:03:04.909 --> 01:03:05.977
Okay, yes.

647
01:03:05.977 --> 01:03:15.057
So right now you've got a picture book in front of you with pictures from your childhood.

648
01:03:15.057 --> 01:03:20.932
And you were explaining that in the pictures these family members . . .

649
01:03:20.932 --> 01:03:23.602
<v SIMS>Were on my dad side.

650
01:03:23.602 --> 01:03:30.545
So color didn't have no meaning to me as a black and white,

651
01:03:30.545 --> 01:03:39.091
because the pictures you're looking at, this is my dad's twin sister and his other siblings.

652
01:03:39.091 --> 01:03:42.830
They are as white as you are.

653
01:03:42.830 --> 01:03:47.636
So color didn't mean black and white to me.

654
01:03:47.636 --> 01:03:53.511
<v COOPER>And then I also asked you when . . . The camera wasn't recording,

655
01:03:53.511 --> 01:04:09.000
but I had asked you earlier if this was the same experience in Mossville as a safe haven versus when you left Mossville how it changed for you.

656
01:04:09.000 --> 01:04:19.968
<v SIMS>What I took from Mossville as a community is that they embraced you and accepted you for who you were.

657
01:04:19.968 --> 01:04:32.581
I saw true value of family—husbands and wives had children, had good jobs, used their money wisely to have nice cars, nice homes.

658
01:04:32.581 --> 01:04:43.000
I took that to know that no matter where you come from, or the situation that you've come through,

659
01:04:43.000 --> 01:04:50.677
you can make your own destiny if you chose to do the right things.

660
01:04:50.677 --> 01:05:01.097
<v COOPER>Also, you were . . . So some people talk about the importance of voting and the voting rights act that happened,

661
01:05:01.097 --> 01:05:05.484
probably right around when you were actually eighteen.

662
01:05:05.484 --> 01:05:14.258
Do you have any memories of voting for the first time, or what that was like?

663
01:05:14.258 --> 01:05:17.000
<v SIMS>Voting, for me, was non-existent.

664
01:05:17.000 --> 01:05:17.548
Why?

665
01:05:17.548 --> 01:05:20.839
My mother was not a voter.

666
01:05:20.839 --> 01:05:24.129
She had a fourth grade education.

667
01:05:24.129 --> 01:05:34.548
And you knew the only way you could vote was to go to the courthouse and take a test.

668
01:05:34.548 --> 01:05:42.774
How you going to take a test if you only had a fourth grade education?

669
01:05:42.774 --> 01:05:52.097
So the people in my family did not have the education to go to register to vote.

670
01:05:52.097 --> 01:06:04.161
And as I grew older and was in school, and learned what the voting right was all about, I registered to vote,

671
01:06:04.161 --> 01:06:14.581
because I learned that the people that gave their lives for me to be able to have that privilege,

672
01:06:14.581 --> 01:06:25.000
it would be a letdown to them to know I had that right and didn't act on it.

673
01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:33.793
<v COOPER>Do you remember who any of the community leaders in Mossville were when you were growing up?

674
01:06:33.793 --> 01:06:40.000
<v SIMS>The Rigmaidens, the Mosses, the Edwards, the Praters . . . to name a few.

675
01:06:40.000 --> 01:06:48.793
<v COOPER>Do you remember any of the activism that they did, or work they did in the community?

676
01:06:48.793 --> 01:06:52.931
<v SIMS>They all did things that was not broadcast.

677
01:06:52.931 --> 01:07:02.241
I mean, they would give the needy money that only the needy knew they gave it to them.

678
01:07:02.241 --> 01:07:10.517
It was never one of these big old, "I did this for you, little old you."

679
01:07:10.517 --> 01:07:12.586
It wasn't like that.

680
01:07:12.586 --> 01:07:25.517
If they saw the need and the desire in you, they would step up to the plate and give you that opportunity with no recognition.

681
01:07:25.517 --> 01:07:30.690
And that's what I liked about the community of Mossville.

682
01:07:30.690 --> 01:07:36.897
<v COOPER>What do you remember about when Mossville High School was first built?

683
01:07:36.897 --> 01:07:37.414
Do

684
01:07:37.414 --> 01:07:40.000
<c.p>you remember if anybody . . .?

685
01:07:40.000 --> 01:07:43.586
<v SIMS>I wasn't there when it was first built.

686
01:07:43.586 --> 01:07:44.483
<v COOPER>Oh, right.

687
01:07:44.483 --> 01:07:48.069
You moved after it had already been established.

688
01:07:48.069 --> 01:07:53.897
<v SIMS>Well I moved there . . . Like I said, I migrated from Lake Charles there.

689
01:07:53.897 --> 01:07:59.276
So when I got there it was already an established, new building.

690
01:07:59.276 --> 01:08:01.966
For me, it was brand new.

691
01:08:01.966 --> 01:08:02.414
<v COOPER>Okay.

692
01:08:02.414 --> 01:08:05.552
What else did I have on here?

693
01:08:05.552 --> 01:08:14.069
Oh, I did want to ask you . . . So you were born about two years after World War II ended.

694
01:08:14.069 --> 01:08:19.000
Did any of your family members serve in the military?

695
01:08:19.000 --> 01:08:26.803
<v SIMS>On my mom's side, she had two brothers that were in World War II.

696
01:08:26.803 --> 01:08:31.262
My father, no. None on my father's side.

697
01:08:31.262 --> 01:08:38.508
Because like I said, my father was disabled and . . . My uncles were there,

698
01:08:38.508 --> 01:08:44.639
but they never served in the service . . . in the military, no.

699
01:08:44.639 --> 01:08:54.672
<v COOPER>Do you remember anyone talking about World War II at the time, or how it impacted your community?

700
01:08:54.672 --> 01:08:57.459
<v SIMS>My grandfather was a veteran.

701
01:08:57.459 --> 01:09:08.049
I think he was either in World War I. I remember him talking about the war when we all,

702
01:09:08.049 --> 01:09:13.066
as children, get around him and hear his stories.

703
01:09:13.066 --> 01:09:18.082
But that was the only military information we had.

704
01:09:18.082 --> 01:09:28.115
<v COOPER>So how often did you go to the doctor in Mossville, or did the doctor come to you?

705
01:09:28.115 --> 01:09:30.344
How did that work?

706
01:09:30.344 --> 01:09:38.148
<v SIMS>We had to go to a little place in Mossville was called the Clinic.

707
01:09:38.148 --> 01:09:46.508
The nurse would come and they would . . . That's when I found out I was anemic,

708
01:09:46.508 --> 01:09:52.639
because we didn't have no money to go to no doctor.

709
01:09:52.639 --> 01:09:58.213
We did home remedies . . . ear ache, tooth ache, cut foot.

710
01:09:58.213 --> 01:10:01.000
We did home remedies.

711
01:10:01.000 --> 01:10:04.380
<v COOPER>Where was the Clinic in Mossville?

712
01:10:04.380 --> 01:10:06.070
<v SIMS>Old Spanish Trail.

713
01:10:06.070 --> 01:10:10.014
<v COOPER>And do you remember who . . . No, okay.

714
01:10:10.014 --> 01:10:13.958
What home remedies do you remember using?

715
01:10:13.958 --> 01:10:21.282
<v SIMS>[laughs] If you cut your foot real bad, you would use fat meat,

716
01:10:21.282 --> 01:10:29.732
put it on the cut, tie it up, and for whatever reason it would heal.

717
01:10:29.732 --> 01:10:35.366
If you had a tooth ache, you would use clover.

718
01:10:35.366 --> 01:10:38.183
It would ease the pain.

719
01:10:38.183 --> 01:10:45.507
I'm doing it right now because my mouth is sore . . . rinsing it out.

720
01:10:45.507 --> 01:10:47.197
Ear ache—olive oil.

721
01:10:47.197 --> 01:10:57.901
Mom would blow smoke from her cigarette in my ear and somehow or another, it would ease the pain.

722
01:10:57.901 --> 01:11:01.282
That's the degree of my sickness.

723
01:11:01.282 --> 01:11:05.225
I was sickly with the sore throat.

724
01:11:05.225 --> 01:11:08.606
I always had a sore throat.

725
01:11:08.606 --> 01:11:14.239
Gargle with salt and water, and something they call Alum.

726
01:11:14.239 --> 01:11:19.310
You don't swallow it, but it draws everything out.

727
01:11:19.310 --> 01:11:23.254
I mean, you can feel the tightness.

728
01:11:23.254 --> 01:11:23.817
Bitter.

729
01:11:23.817 --> 01:11:28.324
Winter medicine for worms and disease, castor oil.

730
01:11:28.324 --> 01:11:31.141
Worst time of your life.

731
01:11:31.141 --> 01:11:34.521
It was a taste you could

732
01:11:34.521 --> 01:11:44.099
<c.p>never get rid of, but they said you take the castor oil and you suck the lemon,

733
01:11:44.099 --> 01:11:50.859
or you take the castor oil and you drink the orange juice.

734
01:11:50.859 --> 01:11:54.239
We never had orange juice [laughs].

735
01:11:54.239 --> 01:11:57.056
We only had the lemon.

736
01:11:57.056 --> 01:12:01.000
That was a time we dreaded.

737
01:12:01.000 --> 01:12:03.863
<v COOPER>And what was castor oil used to prevent?

738
01:12:03.863 --> 01:12:07.084
<v SIMS>To clean your body out for the winter [laughs].

739
01:12:07.084 --> 01:12:07.442
<v COOPER>Oh.

740
01:12:07.442 --> 01:12:09.947
And you would drink the castor oil?

741
01:12:09.947 --> 01:12:10.305
Oh.

742
01:12:10.305 --> 01:12:11.021
<v SIMS>A tablespoon.

743
01:12:11.021 --> 01:12:16.747
To the day I don't use . . . I don't even like . . . Lipstick is made with castor oil.

744
01:12:16.747 --> 01:12:24.621
I smell mine before I buy it, because if it smells like it I don't want it because it makes me nauseous.

745
01:12:24.621 --> 01:12:29.274
<v COOPER>And so you mentioned you still use the clover for a tooth ache.

746
01:12:29.274 --> 01:12:35.000
Are there other home remedies that your mother taught you that you still use today?

747
01:12:35.000 --> 01:12:39.546
<v SIMS>I still have an ear ache, olive oil.

748
01:12:39.546 --> 01:12:42.388
What else do I use?

749
01:12:42.388 --> 01:12:48.071
We're diagnosed with high blood pressure, my mom and whatever.

750
01:12:48.071 --> 01:12:56.596
A dash of cayenne pepper and water kept in the refrigerator, drink it every morning.

751
01:12:56.596 --> 01:12:57.732
What else?

752
01:12:57.732 --> 01:13:02.279
Gargling, still use the . . . what is that called?

753
01:13:02.279 --> 01:13:05.120
I lost train of thought.

754
01:13:05.120 --> 01:13:10.235
The . . . You use it . . . you use the salt water,

755
01:13:10.235 --> 01:13:17.055
and then you put the . . . I can see it in my mind,

756
01:13:17.055 --> 01:13:25.579
but I can't say it, but I said it before what you . . . It's not grime.

757
01:13:25.579 --> 01:13:32.399
It tastes like grime, but it's . . . It just pulls the infection out,

758
01:13:32.399 --> 01:13:37.514
because I still . . . Like I said, with the cancer

759
01:13:37.514 --> 01:13:51.721
<c.p>that took the top of my mouth out, the infection of my sinuses goes down and a lot of times it’ll settle in my tonsils.

760
01:13:51.721 --> 01:13:57.404
And so I'll use that to gargle and it helps.

761
01:13:57.404 --> 01:14:00.814
Because I'm not a medicine person.

762
01:14:00.814 --> 01:14:05.929
I'm not. [Sims clarifies this was called alum water]

763
01:14:05.929 --> 01:14:16.158
<v COOPER>Do you remember if there were medicine people, or healers, in the neighborhood that you ever heard of?

764
01:14:16.158 --> 01:14:19.000
<v SIMS>Not in Mossville, no.

765
01:14:19.000 --> 01:14:22.573
<v COOPER>And where were babies delivered in Mossville?

766
01:14:22.573 --> 01:14:29.208
<v SIMS>During the time I . . . I don't . . . Like back in the day with them,

767
01:14:29.208 --> 01:14:33.802
I'm sure they had a . . . what they call them?

768
01:14:33.802 --> 01:14:34.823
A midwife.

769
01:14:34.823 --> 01:14:45.542
During my era, Davis Charity Hospital was here, so my baby sister was born in Charity Hospital, so was my brother.

770
01:14:45.542 --> 01:14:53.198
<v COOPER>Do you remember . . . You don't remember who any of the local midwives might have been?

771
01:14:53.198 --> 01:14:53.708
Okay.

772
01:14:53.708 --> 01:14:57.281
What . . . So did your family attend church?

773
01:14:57.281 --> 01:15:04.427
I know you mentioned that that was the original reason you moved to Mossville.

774
01:15:04.427 --> 01:15:04.938
<v SIMS>Yes.

775
01:15:04.938 --> 01:15:08.000
<v COOPER>Where did you attend church?

776
01:15:08.000 --> 01:15:10.736
<v SIMS>St. Paul and Mount Nebo.

777
01:15:10.736 --> 01:15:12.925
That's in . . . yes, Mossville.

778
01:15:12.925 --> 01:15:16.208
<v COOPER>And who made up the congregation?

779
01:15:16.208 --> 01:15:19.491
<v SIMS>I called Della, she told me.

780
01:15:19.491 --> 01:15:29.340
Carl Rigmaiden . . . The Rigmaidens: Mr. Carl and his wife Emma, their two children, Carl Ray Junior and Caroline.

781
01:15:29.340 --> 01:15:37.547
The Mosses: Jack Moss, Enola Moss, the wife, Cathy and Sharon Moss, the two girls.

782
01:15:37.547 --> 01:15:46.849
And the Hartman's: Ms. Lorraine and Mr. Hartman—never knew his first name—and their daughters Patsy and Dorothy.

783
01:15:46.849 --> 01:15:48.491
That was it.

784
01:15:48.491 --> 01:15:51.226
And the Beverly clan [laughs].

785
01:15:51.226 --> 01:15:59.434
My uncle had six children, and his wife, and my mom, and her five children.

786
01:15:59.434 --> 01:16:01.623
So that was us.

787
01:16:01.623 --> 01:16:06.000
<v COOPER>And so you would attend every week?

788
01:16:06.000 --> 01:16:06.971
<v SIMS>Oh, yes.

789
01:16:06.971 --> 01:16:08.429
You better [laughs].

790
01:16:08.429 --> 01:16:13.286
We sung in the choir, and we had church functions.

791
01:16:13.286 --> 01:16:14.743
We was there.

792
01:16:14.743 --> 01:16:20.086
<v COOPER>And who were some of the church leaders that you remember?

793
01:16:20.086 --> 01:16:30.286
<v SIMS>Well, the Hartman . . . Mr. Hartman, Mr. Rigmaiden, and Mr. Moss, because it was small and everybody had to do their part.

794
01:16:30.286 --> 01:16:33.686
<v COOPER>What were the women's roles in church?

795
01:16:33.686 --> 01:16:34.171
<v SIMS>Same.

796
01:16:34.171 --> 01:16:38.057
They was . . . What they had . . . Deacons and deaconesses.

797
01:16:38.057 --> 01:16:43.886
And so the ladies were deaconesses and the men was the deacons.

798
01:16:43.886 --> 01:16:47.286
We had prayer service, people that prayed.

799
01:16:47.286 --> 01:16:50.686
It's where I get my praying from.

800
01:16:50.686 --> 01:16:52.629
I'm a prayer warrior.

801
01:16:52.629 --> 01:16:58.457
You heard them pray and it became a spiritual part of you.

802
01:16:58.457 --> 01:17:02.343
We didn't have healing and all of that.

803
01:17:02.343 --> 01:17:05.743
We just had prayer warriors and singers.

804
01:17:05.743 --> 01:17:07.200
They served communion.

805
01:17:07.200 --> 01:17:10.114
Certain people can serve communion—the ladies.

806
01:17:10.114 --> 01:17:14.000
They wore white, a white hat on.

807
01:17:14.000 --> 01:17:18.167
<v COOPER>And you mentioned that you were in the choir.

808
01:17:18.167 --> 01:17:21.407
How else were you involved in church?

809
01:17:21.407 --> 01:17:21.870
<v SIMS>Speaking.

810
01:17:21.870 --> 01:17:27.426
If we would have to do a drama, I would do that.

811
01:17:27.426 --> 01:17:31.130
I was anything they wanted me to do.

812
01:17:31.130 --> 01:17:37.148
<v COOPER>Also, there was a post office for Mossville that was established in 1915.

813
01:17:37.148 --> 01:17:41.315
Do you know how long it was in operation?

814
01:17:41.315 --> 01:17:47.333
<v SIMS>I don't know the in and outs of the old things of Mossville.

815
01:17:47.333 --> 01:17:57.519
I just lived there and became a part of the . . . But never, nothing to the degree of learning the history of anything.

816
01:17:57.519 --> 01:18:04.000
<v COOPER>Do you remember where the post office was when you were growing up?

817
01:18:04.000 --> 01:18:04.358
<v SIMS>Yes.

818
01:18:04.358 --> 01:18:08.654
But I couldn't tell you the name of the street or whatever.

819
01:18:08.654 --> 01:18:13.309
<v COOPER>Do you remember if there was a library that you would go to?

820
01:18:13.309 --> 01:18:14.025
<v SIMS>School library.

821
01:18:14.025 --> 01:18:15.099
<v COOPER>The school library.

822
01:18:15.099 --> 01:18:19.395
Do you know if Mossville was ever officially incorporated into a town?

823
01:18:19.395 --> 01:18:20.469
<v SIMS>I don't know.

824
01:18:20.469 --> 01:18:21.185
<v COOPER>No [laughs].

825
01:18:21.185 --> 01:18:21.543
Okay.

826
01:18:21.543 --> 01:18:24.765
What are some of your fondest memories of Mossville?

827
01:18:24.765 --> 01:18:27.630
I know you talked a lot about school.

828
01:18:27.630 --> 01:18:33.000
Is there anything else that you would want to share about growing up there?

829
01:18:33.000 --> 01:18:35.097
<v SIMS>That's all I knew.

830
01:18:35.097 --> 01:18:40.865
The school was my . . . I mean, as far as the community,

831
01:18:40.865 --> 01:18:47.682
the community made up the people and my only connection was the school.

832
01:18:47.682 --> 01:18:53.449
You never went to just walking and go to somebody's house.

833
01:18:53.449 --> 01:18:58.169
You just went to school that was their children.

834
01:18:58.169 --> 01:19:02.363
You had a program, you met the parents.

835
01:19:02.363 --> 01:19:07.607
You went to church, you met the parents, the children.

836
01:19:07.607 --> 01:19:10.228
That was my life there.

837
01:19:10.228 --> 01:19:10.753
Yes.

838
01:19:10.753 --> 01:19:14.423
To this day, I'm not a groupie.

839
01:19:14.423 --> 01:19:22.288
I know you, you're my friend, everybody's my friend, but I'm not a telephone person,

840
01:19:22.288 --> 01:19:28.581
I'm not a knock on the door . . . I know all my neighbors,

841
01:19:28.581 --> 01:19:33.824
but we . . . I'm not in and out of their houses.

842
01:19:33.824 --> 01:19:37.494
And that's the way I came up.

843
01:19:37.494 --> 01:19:45.360
<v COOPER>So I know you talked a little bit about how important fashion was to you.

844
01:19:45.360 --> 01:19:46.408
What do

845
01:19:46.408 --> 01:19:51.127
<c.p>you remember about the fashion growing up in Mossville?

846
01:19:51.127 --> 01:19:59.517
<v SIMS>The greatest fashion we had, that I wanted so bad to be in . . . When school started,

847
01:19:59.517 --> 01:20:06.858
the children that had . . . all got to wear new clothes in the latest fashion.

848
01:20:06.858 --> 01:20:15.772
There was some black ankle boots, laced up the front and tied, that all the girls had.

849
01:20:15.772 --> 01:20:20.491
And I wanted so bad to have a pair.

850
01:20:20.491 --> 01:20:22.588
I had a pair.

851
01:20:22.588 --> 01:20:27.307
I wore them until they couldn't be worn anymore.

852
01:20:27.307 --> 01:20:40.940
I don't know how I got them, who bought them for me, but that was the time I remembered of having something new when school opened.

853
01:20:40.940 --> 01:20:45.659
Other than that, everything else was hand me downs.

854
01:20:45.659 --> 01:20:48.281
But I remember those shoes.

855
01:20:48.281 --> 01:20:51.427
I wore those shoes every day.

856
01:20:51.427 --> 01:20:53.000
Every day.

857
01:20:53.000 --> 01:20:56.797
<v COOPER>And I was going to ask you . . . Oh,

858
01:20:56.797 --> 01:21:11.034
also . . . Because you mentioned when you went to the convention that you were "dressed to kill"—is the phrase you used—and you said that was part of your culture growing up.

859
01:21:11.034 --> 01:21:15.780
What was it like dressing up for events in Mossville?

860
01:21:15.780 --> 01:21:19.102
What would people wear to family gatherings?

861
01:21:19.102 --> 01:21:21.475
<v SIMS>They would wear nice clothes.

862
01:21:21.475 --> 01:21:32.390
When they asked me to be the queen in the eleventh grade, and I'm going, "How am I going to be the queen?

863
01:21:32.390 --> 01:21:34.763
I don't have no gown.

864
01:21:34.763 --> 01:21:37.136
I don't have no shoes."

865
01:21:37.136 --> 01:21:42.831
"Oh yeah, Audrey, we want you to be the eleventh grade queen."

866
01:21:42.831 --> 01:21:47.102
That was the year I twirled with the fire.

867
01:21:47.102 --> 01:21:53.271
The P.E. teacher bought the gold material and the fire baton for me.

868
01:21:53.271 --> 01:21:55.644
My mom made the outfit.

869
01:21:55.644 --> 01:21:58.492
It was a swimsuit she made.

870
01:21:58.492 --> 01:21:59.441
No pattern.

871
01:21:59.441 --> 01:22:07.508
She also bought me some gold ballerina slippers that I went on the field and twirled in.

872
01:22:07.508 --> 01:22:18.898
But I had to do the inauguration of being Ms. Junior to walk. [Pier?], the queen before me, she and I were great friends.

873
01:22:18.898 --> 01:22:23.169
She said, "Audrey, you're going to wear my dress."

874
01:22:23.169 --> 01:22:28.864
Now [Pier?] . . . I'm like a size ten. [Pier?] must have been a

875
01:22:28.864 --> 01:22:29.339
<c.p>fourteen.

876
01:22:29.339 --> 01:22:39.305
But my mom took the dress, fitted me, and it was wide and I had to have can cans on it.

877
01:22:39.305 --> 01:22:43.576
Everybody that had a can-can gave me a can-can.

878
01:22:43.576 --> 01:22:47.847
Dianne [Cotlong?] gave me the white shoes to wear.

879
01:22:47.847 --> 01:22:55.915
And to this day I feel so embarrassed to say I had big feet for my height.

880
01:22:55.915 --> 01:22:58.288
And I stretched her shoes.

881
01:22:58.288 --> 01:23:05.881
I know she could never wear them again [laughs] when I gave them back to her.

882
01:23:05.881 --> 01:23:13.000
But that's how I walked to represent the eleventh grade, in my classmates' clothes.

883
01:23:13.000 --> 01:23:21.325
<v COOPER>Did you ever get to go to a wedding in Mossville, or a big community event?

884
01:23:21.325 --> 01:23:21.846
No?

885
01:23:21.846 --> 01:23:24.447
Were there ever any funerals?

886
01:23:24.447 --> 01:23:24.967
<v SIMS>Yes.

887
01:23:24.967 --> 01:23:28.610
Funerals, classmates' funerals when school was out.

888
01:23:28.610 --> 01:23:32.772
Like I said, transportation was not our thing.

889
01:23:32.772 --> 01:23:34.854
Either bus or walk.

890
01:23:34.854 --> 01:23:38.496
Mom had no car and wasn't driving.

891
01:23:38.496 --> 01:23:48.902
The bus would come from Lake Charles there to pick her up, bring her across the bridge, bring her back.

892
01:23:48.902 --> 01:23:57.748
She would catch a ride when she worked at Shay’s with the ladies that worked at Shay’s.

893
01:23:57.748 --> 01:24:05.033
So travelling assessment to one place to another was not an option for us.

894
01:24:05.033 --> 01:24:10.236
Either we went by the school bus, or you walked.

895
01:24:10.236 --> 01:24:17.000
<v COOPER>And so you weren't able to attend the funerals because of transportation?

896
01:24:17.000 --> 01:24:18.728
<v SIMS>Yes, a lot of times.

897
01:24:18.728 --> 01:24:25.296
Or if somebody knew I wanted to go and they were going they would ask like they do now.

898
01:24:25.296 --> 01:24:31.864
Like when I go to Mossville, they know I don't drive across the lake so they'll call, "We're going.

899
01:24:31.864 --> 01:24:33.247
You want to come?"

900
01:24:33.247 --> 01:24:35.321
They still do that with me.

901
01:24:35.321 --> 01:24:40.160
<v COOPER>Who made the decisions in your family, like the decisions about school and where

902
01:24:40.160 --> 01:24:41.543
<c.p>you went, and discipline?

903
01:24:41.543 --> 01:24:42.235
<v SIMS>My mama.

904
01:24:42.235 --> 01:24:45.000
<v COOPER>How did she discipline you growing up?

905
01:24:45.000 --> 01:24:46.483
<v SIMS>She didn't whip.

906
01:24:46.483 --> 01:24:47.471
She punished.

907
01:24:47.471 --> 01:24:54.885
My greatest fear was if I did something and Roy would come home from college,

908
01:24:54.885 --> 01:25:04.276
I couldn't cross the bridge to go see him, and he couldn't cross the bridge to come see me.

909
01:25:04.276 --> 01:25:09.218
So I knew, and I knew Momma would find out.

910
01:25:09.218 --> 01:25:22.069
That's why all the moms wanted me to be with their daughters because they knew they could depend on me to do the right thing [laughs].

911
01:25:22.069 --> 01:25:28.000
<v COOPER>Do you ever remember any conflicts over decision making between your parents?

912
01:25:28.000 --> 01:25:35.908
<v SIMS>Like I said, Mom raised us basically on our own, so there was never any argument.

913
01:25:35.908 --> 01:25:38.874
That's why I don't like argument.

914
01:25:38.874 --> 01:25:41.839
I didn't . . . I wasn't around it.

915
01:25:41.839 --> 01:25:47.276
My uncle and my aunt, they weren't argument people, fighting people.

916
01:25:47.276 --> 01:25:54.195
So I have no tolerance for it because I wasn't brought up with it.

917
01:25:54.195 --> 01:26:05.069
<v COOPER>Did you . . . Do you feel like you treated your own children growing up the same that your mother treated you growing up?

918
01:26:05.069 --> 01:26:11.000
Were there similarities or differences between the way you were raised?

919
01:26:11.000 --> 01:26:13.306
<v SIMS>I didn't cheat them.

920
01:26:13.306 --> 01:26:16.188
In fact, I gave them.

921
01:26:16.188 --> 01:26:27.718
They knew, and my daughter can tell you now, on Saturday mornings you woke up and you did your work.

922
01:26:27.718 --> 01:26:38.094
You cleaned the house and did everything you had to do before you went out and had fun.

923
01:26:38.094 --> 01:26:43.859
I brought those principles in with me: honor, respect, obedience.

924
01:26:43.859 --> 01:26:47.894
I brought that in to my household.

925
01:26:47.894 --> 01:26:56.541
I did not talk back to my mom, even after having two children and married.

926
01:26:56.541 --> 01:27:02.882
My children do not talk back to me, no matter what.

927
01:27:02.882 --> 01:27:13.259
They might not agree, they might not like it, but they know that I discipline them with love,

928
01:27:13.259 --> 01:27:20.753
and try to tell them the truth and the right way to go.

929
01:27:20.753 --> 01:27:24.788
All I can do is tell them.

930
01:27:24.788 --> 01:27:29.400
All I can do is pray for them.

931
01:27:29.400 --> 01:27:33.435
They have to make their own choices.

932
01:27:33.435 --> 01:27:38.624
<v COOPER>How were unmarried people viewed upon in the community?

933
01:27:38.624 --> 01:27:42.082
<v SIMS>I heard my mom say it.

934
01:27:42.082 --> 01:27:46.694
My mom was not a runaround woman either.

935
01:27:46.694 --> 01:27:49.000
She raised us alone.

936
01:27:49.000 --> 01:27:54.765
We didn't see men in and out of our house.

937
01:27:54.765 --> 01:27:59.953
If she had a man, that was her man.

938
01:27:59.953 --> 01:28:04.565
And when he was gone, he was gone.

939
01:28:04.565 --> 01:28:13.212
But Mom said that the married couples would look at single moms or women differently,

940
01:28:13.212 --> 01:28:21.282
and you had to . . . That's why she was so adamant about men respecting you.

941
01:28:21.282 --> 01:28:28.200
You put them in their place because you have the last say.

942
01:28:28.200 --> 01:28:33.965
And if they don't respect you, they're breaking the law.

943
01:28:33.965 --> 01:28:38.000
That's what I grew up with.

944
01:28:38.000 --> 01:28:42.226
<v COOPER>When did she meet your step father?

945
01:28:42.226 --> 01:28:43.434
<v SIMS>Oh, Lord.

946
01:28:43.434 --> 01:28:57.925
Before I married Roy, while I was going with Roy because I was with Roy before my stepfather, so it had to be '62.

947
01:28:57.925 --> 01:29:00.943
<v COOPER>And how did they meet?

948
01:29:00.943 --> 01:29:02.755
Do you remember?

949
01:29:02.755 --> 01:29:05.170
<v SIMS>My aunt’s cousin [laughs].

950
01:29:05.170 --> 01:29:10.000
<v COOPER>What did your stepfather do for work?

951
01:29:10.000 --> 01:29:15.523
<v SIMS>Went to prison [laughs] . . . because he was come from prison.

952
01:29:15.523 --> 01:29:16.628
He stole.

953
01:29:16.628 --> 01:29:24.912
The experience I had with that was very traumatic and showed how strong I was.

954
01:29:24.912 --> 01:29:27.674
I didn't even realize it.

955
01:29:27.674 --> 01:29:37.615
He had robbed a . . . not a bank . . . I don't know if it was a filler station or whatever.

956
01:29:37.615 --> 01:29:43.690
And he came in the house and he had a gun.

957
01:29:43.690 --> 01:29:52.527
I'm the oldest in the house with my sisters and my brothers and he tells me,

958
01:29:52.527 --> 01:30:06.335
"I have this gun and if the police come and ask you do you know me, you tell them no because I have this gun."

959
01:30:06.335 --> 01:30:10.201
He went and hid in the house.

960
01:30:10.201 --> 01:30:15.172
The police did come, knocked on the front door.

961
01:30:15.172 --> 01:30:20.142
My mom's at work over here in Lake Charles.

962
01:30:20.142 --> 01:30:22.904
"Do you know Saul Johnson?"

963
01:30:22.904 --> 01:30:24.008
"No sir."

964
01:30:24.008 --> 01:30:26.218
A lot of questions.

965
01:30:26.218 --> 01:30:36.159
But I'm trying to keep my cool, my sanity, my strength to protect my brothers and my sisters.

966
01:30:36.159 --> 01:30:38.368
When the policemen left,

967
01:30:38.368 --> 01:30:52.728
he ran out the back door and jumped in his car and the police started chasing him and captured him on the way to DeQuincy [Louisiana],

968
01:30:52.728 --> 01:30:56.042
because that's where his mom was.

969
01:30:56.042 --> 01:31:02.669
I had to leave, go to the neighbor's house, call my momma,

970
01:31:02.669 --> 01:31:10.954
let her know what had just happened, and then I broke down and started crying.

971
01:31:10.954 --> 01:31:22.000
To me, that's traumatic . . . that I didn't break down when the police knocked on the door and confronted me.

972
01:31:22.000 --> 01:31:22.415
<v COOPER>Wow.

973
01:31:22.415 --> 01:31:27.815
So you really were in charge of your brothers and sisters growing up.

974
01:31:27.815 --> 01:31:28.231
<v SIMS>Yes.

975
01:31:28.231 --> 01:31:31.554
I was like a second mom to them.

976
01:31:31.554 --> 01:31:33.631
<v COOPER>Even after you were married?

977
01:31:33.631 --> 01:31:42.769
<v SIMS>[Agrees] Because when I got a job, I bought them their school clothes so they had clothes the first day of school.

978
01:31:42.769 --> 01:31:43.600
I did.

979
01:31:43.600 --> 01:31:46.508
<v COOPER>What was your wedding like with Roy?

980
01:31:46.508 --> 01:31:49.000
Was it in Lake Charles?

981
01:31:49.000 --> 01:31:51.391
<v SIMS>It was in Lake Charles.

982
01:31:51.391 --> 01:31:53.304
I made my gown.

983
01:31:53.304 --> 01:32:04.304
I made my bridesmaids' gowns, which was my two cousins and my classmate, Thibodeaux—I don't know if you're going to interview her—Bonnie Lou.

984
01:32:04.304 --> 01:32:12.435
My uncle, bless his soul, he and his wife bought my flowers because I couldn't afford that.

985
01:32:12.435 --> 01:32:20.087
Roy's brother, we had the reunion at their house in the kitchen and the dining room.

986
01:32:20.087 --> 01:32:25.348
My uncle was the pastor of the church that we married.

987
01:32:25.348 --> 01:32:31.565
We were one of the first ones to get married in the church.

988
01:32:31.565 --> 01:32:34.913
So it was like a historical thing.

989
01:32:34.913 --> 01:32:35.870
It was.

990
01:32:35.870 --> 01:32:38.261
<v COOPER>What was the celebration like?

991
01:32:38.261 --> 01:32:41.609
Do you remember what it looked like?

992
01:32:41.609 --> 01:32:46.870
<v SIMS>Oh, I was just too excited to be married to remember.

993
01:32:46.870 --> 01:32:48.783
But it was nice.

994
01:32:48.783 --> 01:32:52.609
His brother, the older . . . not the oldest one,

995
01:32:52.609 --> 01:33:00.261
but one of the older brothers, because he [Roy] was the baby of like thirteen children,

996
01:33:00.261 --> 01:33:10.304
he paid for us a room at a little motel in town for the night for us to be a honeymoon.

997
01:33:10.304 --> 01:33:17.000
It was over at twelve o'clock [noon] because we had to go [laughs].

998
01:33:17.000 --> 01:33:18.900
<v COOPER>So I'm curious, too.

999
01:33:18.900 --> 01:33:24.600
You mentioned that your father was a musician and he played zydeco.

1000
01:33:24.600 --> 01:33:27.925
How did . . . Where did that come from?

1001
01:33:27.925 --> 01:33:30.300
Did he learn that from . . .?

1002
01:33:30.300 --> 01:33:31.725
<v SIMS>From the streets.

1003
01:33:31.725 --> 01:33:32.200
<v COOPER>Okay.

1004
01:33:32.200 --> 01:33:34.100
<v SIMS>From being in Crowley.

1005
01:33:34.100 --> 01:33:45.975
The [Dugas?], [Marcel Dugas?] was the band. [And Marcel Dugas?], doing the research of my mom's side, her sister was married to Mr. Dugas' brother.

1006
01:33:45.975 --> 01:33:55.000
I guess my father, being a street child, liked entertainment and he picked up a lot of musical skills.

1007
01:33:55.000 --> 01:34:05.450
They embraced him because I think that's how my mom and he met is through that . . . That was my mom's momma's sister.

1008
01:34:05.450 --> 01:34:07.350
That's how they met.

1009
01:34:07.350 --> 01:34:14.000
<v COOPER>And did he ever teach you or your siblings how to play music?

1010
01:34:14.000 --> 01:34:14.843
<v SIMS>I wish.

1011
01:34:14.843 --> 01:34:17.792
The harmonica, he could play the harmonica.

1012
01:34:17.792 --> 01:34:23.691
And to the day I wonder why didn't I ask him to teach me?

1013
01:34:23.691 --> 01:34:25.376
Because he would have.

1014
01:34:25.376 --> 01:34:31.697
But I have an ear for music; I sing, I did a little piano lessons.

1015
01:34:31.697 --> 01:34:36.753
But the music is really my son's second generation picked it up.

1016
01:34:36.753 --> 01:34:41.809
<v COOPER>You would have piano lessons when you were growing up in Mossville?

1017
01:34:41.809 --> 01:34:42.652
No? [laughs]

1018
01:34:42.652 --> 01:34:46.444
<v SIMS>My aunt was doing it for a little while.

1019
01:34:46.444 --> 01:34:49.393
But like I said, everything was money.

1020
01:34:49.393 --> 01:34:57.820
<c.p>And my mom couldn't . . . I think I might have took maybe about a month of it or whatever it was,

1021
01:34:57.820 --> 01:35:01.612
just enough . . . Like I said, I was musically inclined.

1022
01:35:01.612 --> 01:35:08.775
I could do the boogie-woogie and I could listen to something . . . I never learned to read music,

1023
01:35:08.775 --> 01:35:12.567
but I could listen to something and play it.

1024
01:35:12.567 --> 01:35:16.360
If I heard it once, I could play it.

1025
01:35:16.360 --> 01:35:18.466
But I never pursued it.

1026
01:35:18.466 --> 01:35:22.680
Had so many other things going on with me [laughs].

1027
01:35:22.680 --> 01:35:24.365
It wasn't a passion.

1028
01:35:24.365 --> 01:35:29.000
<v COOPER>Did you ever get to see your dad play live?

1029
01:35:29.000 --> 01:35:29.889
<v SIMS>Oh yes.

1030
01:35:29.889 --> 01:35:32.111
<v COOPER>Where would you watch him?

1031
01:35:32.111 --> 01:35:34.778
<v SIMS>In Lake Charles in night clubs.

1032
01:35:34.778 --> 01:35:35.667
Oh yes.

1033
01:35:35.667 --> 01:35:40.111
<v COOPER>Did you ever hear about any night clubs in Mossville?

1034
01:35:40.111 --> 01:35:41.444
<v SIMS>Oh yes [laughs].

1035
01:35:41.444 --> 01:35:45.444
The . . . what . . . God, what's . . . I can't think of that.

1036
01:35:45.444 --> 01:35:54.778
I mean, my mind right now . . . my mind is like seventy, retired. [laughs] Joy Hill that was one of the places.

1037
01:35:54.778 --> 01:36:01.000
But like I said, being a pastor's niece and all, we didn't club [laughs].

1038
01:36:01.000 --> 01:36:06.778
I did the sock hops, but that was just on the weekend . . . sodas.

1039
01:36:06.778 --> 01:36:08.111
But oh no.

1040
01:36:08.111 --> 01:36:13.000
<v COOPER>What did you know about that when you were growing up?

1041
01:36:13.000 --> 01:36:17.000
I think . . . Some people mentioned the Paradise Club.

1042
01:36:17.000 --> 01:36:17.562
<v SIMS>Yes.

1043
01:36:17.562 --> 01:36:20.938
Paradise . . . Yes, you know the names.

1044
01:36:20.938 --> 01:36:31.625
I mean, you heard the names but if you're not a part of that, it don't stay with you.

1045
01:36:31.625 --> 01:36:33.312
We knew, yes.

1046
01:36:33.312 --> 01:36:39.500
<v COOPER>Did your father's band ever play at any of the clubs?

1047
01:36:39.500 --> 01:36:40.625
<v SIMS>Oh no.

1048
01:36:40.625 --> 01:36:41.188
<v COOPER>No?

1049
01:36:41.188 --> 01:36:43.438
<v SIMS>No, that's too small.

1050
01:36:43.438 --> 01:36:46.250
Yes, they would play . . . See,

1051
01:36:46.250 --> 01:36:58.625
zydeco in the day when I came up was not cool because we come from a culture that the down east people,

1052
01:36:58.625 --> 01:37:05.938
they had this accent and if you talked French, they laughed at you.

1053
01:37:05.938 --> 01:37:09.312
So they wouldn't teach us French.

1054
01:37:09.312 --> 01:37:14.375
And we didn't zydeco because they laughed at you.

1055
01:37:14.375 --> 01:37:21.125
So yes, my father played that, but they played it down east.

1056
01:37:21.125 --> 01:37:23.938
Lake Charles is the city.

1057
01:37:23.938 --> 01:37:25.625
Too much culture.

1058
01:37:25.625 --> 01:37:33.500
That's when you heard zydeco. [phone ringing] Who is that that keeps calling me?

1059
01:37:33.500 --> 01:37:35.188
That's my granddaughter.

1060
01:37:35.188 --> 01:37:38.000
Yes, who is that?

1061
01:37:38.000 --> 01:37:38.349
<v COOPER>Alright.

1062
01:37:38.349 --> 01:37:40.444
<v SIMS>I thought . . . I didn't realize it.

1063
01:37:40.444 --> 01:37:42.889
<v COOPER>Yes, we can . . . We'll totally wrap up.

1064
01:37:42.889 --> 01:37:51.619
I just have a few more questions for you and then we'll come back a different time and ask all of the rest of them.

1065
01:37:51.619 --> 01:37:56.159
So you mentioned that you would press other people's hair in high school.

1066
01:37:56.159 --> 01:37:57.905
How did you do that?

1067
01:37:57.905 --> 01:37:59.302
What would you use?

1068
01:37:59.302 --> 01:38:01.397
<v SIMS>A straightening iron and a stove.

1069
01:38:01.397 --> 01:38:08.730
They would sit down between my legs if they were little girls, or if not I'd stand and press their hair,

1070
01:38:08.730 --> 01:38:13.968
my classmates, my cousins, the little girls in the neighborhood, and I wouldn't burn them.

1071
01:38:13.968 --> 01:38:17.111
My mom taught me, because she could do hair.

1072
01:38:17.111 --> 01:38:21.302
<v COOPER>So she would do your siblings' and your hair too growing up?

1073
01:38:21.302 --> 01:38:22.000
<v SIMS>Yes.

1074
01:38:22.000 --> 01:38:26.250
<v COOPER>Would you . . . Where would you ever go to get your hair cut?

1075
01:38:26.250 --> 01:38:28.729
Would she do that too for you?

1076
01:38:28.729 --> 01:38:29.083
<v SIMS>Yes.

1077
01:38:29.083 --> 01:38:31.917
We didn't go . . . no money. [laughs] That costs.

1078
01:38:31.917 --> 01:38:38.292
<v COOPER>And then also, did you ever hear any stories from your father about playing in the night clubs?

1079
01:38:38.292 --> 01:38:41.833
Do you remember anything that he told you about that?

1080
01:38:41.833 --> 01:38:42.188
No?

1081
01:38:42.188 --> 01:38:45.375
Okay, well I think that this has been wonderful.

1082
01:38:45.375 --> 01:38:48.208
You have shared so many stories with us.

1083
01:38:48.208 --> 01:38:53.167
Is there anything else you . . . that's on your mind that you want to share . . .

1084
01:38:53.167 --> 01:38:53.521
<v SIMS>No.

1085
01:38:53.521 --> 01:38:55.292
<v COOPER>. . . today before we wrap up?

1086
01:38:55.292 --> 01:38:56.000
No?

1087
01:38:56.000 --> 01:39:01.083
<v SIMS>I have a . . . Like I said, I have a book of memories, because I write

1088
01:39:01.083 --> 01:39:03.116
<c.p>everything and I remember so much.

1089
01:39:03.116 --> 01:39:08.537
But you have to . . . It's in me, and you have to . . . and then I can relate.

1090
01:39:08.537 --> 01:39:08.876
<v COOPER>Yes.

1091
01:39:08.876 --> 01:39:13.959
Well it was wonderful speaking to you and learning more about your life and Mossville.

1092
01:39:13.959 --> 01:39:17.347
And yes, on behalf of the museum, the Calcasieu Museum,

1093
01:39:17.347 --> 01:39:24.124
and LSU we just want to say thank you so much for sharing with us and agreeing to be interviewed.

1094
01:39:24.124 --> 01:39:27.512
And with that, I'd like to close up this interview.

1095
01:39:27.512 --> 01:39:33.950
But I'll let you know . . . We'll talk with you about coming back maybe and doing a follow up interview.

1096
01:39:33.950 --> 01:39:35.983
But yes, thank you so much.

1097
01:39:35.983 --> 01:39:37.000
<v SIMS>You're welcome.

1098
01:39:37.000 --> 01:39:37.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4550.

1099
01:39:37.429 --> 01:39:37.857
End Session I.]
